1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:18,118
Welcome to <i>Chef & My Fridge,</i>
with Lee Seon-min and CODE KUNST.

2
00:00:18,184 --> 00:00:21,955
COKUN is infamous as a light eater
in the entertainment business

3
00:00:22,022 --> 00:00:24,190
-just as infamous as Joo Woo-jae.
-Yes.

4
00:00:24,257 --> 00:00:25,925
-That little?
-We're probably the same.

5
00:00:25,992 --> 00:00:26,926
Do you not eat much?

6
00:00:26,993 --> 00:00:28,094
I've been eating more recently.

7
00:00:28,161 --> 00:00:29,195
Do you eat more now?

8
00:00:29,262 --> 00:00:31,331
It's been a year
since I started eating normally.

9
00:00:32,665 --> 00:00:34,701
This might be good news for them.

10
00:00:34,768 --> 00:00:35,969
I always eat red meat now.

11
00:00:36,036 --> 00:00:39,105
Don't most people eat red meat
about once or twice a week?

12
00:00:39,172 --> 00:00:40,840
I mean, even in soups and stews.

13
00:00:40,907 --> 00:00:42,842
-What did he eat before?
-It's not always grilled.

14
00:00:42,909 --> 00:00:46,146
I usually stuck to eggs or chicken,

15
00:00:46,212 --> 00:00:48,381
but recently,
I've been eating more pork and beef.

16
00:00:48,948 --> 00:00:51,284
Why does he say it
like it's an accomplishment?

17
00:00:51,851 --> 00:00:53,420
That's just normal, COKUN.

18
00:00:53,486 --> 00:00:54,687
It's an improvement for me.

19
00:00:54,754 --> 00:00:55,989
Really?

20
00:00:56,056 --> 00:00:58,124
-Meat usually comes in packs, right?
-Yes.

21
00:00:58,191 --> 00:01:00,560
Then I always make sure
I eat half of it in a day.

22
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:02,629
-Sorry?
-Half a pack?

23
00:01:02,695 --> 00:01:04,397
How much chicken breast do you eat?

24
00:01:04,464 --> 00:01:06,166
I eat two of those.

25
00:01:06,232 --> 00:01:09,069
-Two chicken breasts? That's a lot.
-Really? Then you do eat a lot.

26
00:01:09,135 --> 00:01:10,203
-I guess.
-For a meal?

27
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:11,871
-He must like it.
-No, in a day.

28
00:01:11,938 --> 00:01:13,940
-In a day?
-It wasn't for one meal?

29
00:01:14,007 --> 00:01:16,943
Are you kidding me? In a day?

30
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:19,412
Have you ever seen him eat?

31
00:01:19,479 --> 00:01:20,947
I've seen it on-screen.

32
00:01:21,014 --> 00:01:22,015
HE SAW IT DURING A SHOOT

33
00:01:22,082 --> 00:01:24,484
And it took him 40 minutes to eat.

34
00:01:24,551 --> 00:01:27,220
So it was a little frustrating.

35
00:01:27,287 --> 00:01:29,956
How are you feeling today?
Are you in good condition to eat?

36
00:01:30,023 --> 00:01:31,157
Here's the thing.

37
00:01:31,224 --> 00:01:33,693
The production crew came and took
literally everything

38
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:36,663
-that I had in the fridge.
-We do.

39
00:01:36,729 --> 00:01:39,499
I like stir-fried dried squid,
so I had bought three packs,

40
00:01:39,566 --> 00:01:41,734
thinking they'd probably leave one pack.

41
00:01:41,801 --> 00:01:44,704
But they took all three.

42
00:01:44,771 --> 00:01:47,874
I've been starving since last night,
so yes, I can eat.

43
00:01:48,575 --> 00:01:49,609
You didn't eat yesterday?

44
00:01:49,676 --> 00:01:51,277
Nothing after lunch.

45
00:01:51,578 --> 00:01:52,412
DAZED…

46
00:01:52,645 --> 00:01:54,280
Can you skip meals like that?

47
00:01:54,347 --> 00:01:57,350
-It's unthinkable.
-Is it unthinkable?

48
00:01:57,417 --> 00:01:58,585
-Impossible.
-I see.

49
00:01:58,651 --> 00:01:59,619
BIG EATERS CAN'T UNDERSTAND

50
00:01:59,686 --> 00:02:02,922
Did you say you felt like
you were framed as a light eater?

51
00:02:02,989 --> 00:02:05,191
I guess he has something to explain.

52
00:02:05,258 --> 00:02:07,760
Well, I think I do eat the least
among the people here.

53
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:09,262
AN ISSUE(?) WITH THE CONTROL GROUP

54
00:02:09,329 --> 00:02:12,298
Even when I find something very delicious,

55
00:02:12,365 --> 00:02:15,235
I guess I'm not expressive enough
to show it.

56
00:02:15,301 --> 00:02:16,836
I tried practicing.

57
00:02:16,903 --> 00:02:18,538
-You practiced?
-You did?

58
00:02:18,605 --> 00:02:22,809
I tried to see how my friends who eat well
react after they eat something.

59
00:02:22,876 --> 00:02:23,843
Most of them went…

60
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:24,878
FROWN

61
00:02:24,944 --> 00:02:26,279
They did this often.

62
00:02:26,346 --> 00:02:28,882
Yes, there's a bit of a frown here.

63
00:02:28,948 --> 00:02:31,551
They'd do this
when they ate something really good.

64
00:02:31,618 --> 00:02:34,120
-Wait.
-That looks like he had something bad.

65
00:02:34,187 --> 00:02:37,657
-I plan to do that today if it's good.
-Really?

66
00:02:37,724 --> 00:02:39,392
HOW WILL COKUN REVIEW THE FOOD?

67
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:41,628
COMING UP

68
00:02:41,828 --> 00:02:43,663
TODAY, HE'S A BIG EATER

69
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:44,864
AMAZED

70
00:02:44,931 --> 00:02:45,899
AND AMAZED

71
00:02:46,432 --> 00:02:47,967
COMING UP: COKUN'S GENUINE REACTIONS

72
00:02:48,034 --> 00:02:50,670
-It's so good.
-"It's so good."

73
00:02:51,237 --> 00:02:53,907
When he talked
to our production crew, he said,

74
00:02:53,973 --> 00:02:56,442
"I've bulked up,

75
00:02:56,509 --> 00:02:58,778
so I'm now bigger,
and my stomach got bigger too."

76
00:02:58,845 --> 00:02:59,913
He bulked up?

77
00:02:59,979 --> 00:03:01,281
That's him getting bigger?

78
00:03:01,347 --> 00:03:02,982
Currently, my weight

79
00:03:03,049 --> 00:03:05,919
fluctuates between 71 and 72 kg.

80
00:03:06,619 --> 00:03:07,720
How tall are you?

81
00:03:07,787 --> 00:03:09,789
-I'm 176 cm.
-You're 176 cm?

82
00:03:09,856 --> 00:03:11,858
-That's not bad.
-That's considered healthy.

83
00:03:11,925 --> 00:03:13,893
-I see.
-I was 71 kg

84
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:15,828
when I had to pay
the adult fare at age 13.

85
00:03:16,029 --> 00:03:18,698
SEON-MIN AT 13 = COKUN AT 36

86
00:03:19,098 --> 00:03:19,999
BULKED UP(?) AT WARP SPEED

87
00:03:20,066 --> 00:03:21,801
-He was 71 kg at 13?
-When the driver stopped you?

88
00:03:21,868 --> 00:03:24,504
So you bulked up and reached 71 kg?

89
00:03:24,571 --> 00:03:26,906
I planned my weight gain over three years.

90
00:03:26,973 --> 00:03:29,209
-How old were you back then?
-I was 60 kg.

91
00:03:29,275 --> 00:03:30,977
-You were 60 kg?
-He gained a lot.

92
00:03:31,044 --> 00:03:33,446
-Ten kilograms.
-I can see more meat on his face.

93
00:03:33,513 --> 00:03:34,647
When he was on a soccer show…

94
00:03:34,714 --> 00:03:38,651
-I was 60 kg back then.
-Back then, he looked way too thin.

95
00:03:38,718 --> 00:03:39,786
He looked like this.

96
00:03:40,019 --> 00:03:41,588
A HUMAN PEN(?)

97
00:03:41,654 --> 00:03:43,122
Do you plan to bulk up even more?

98
00:03:43,189 --> 00:03:44,757
-My goal is 75 kg.
-It's 75 kg.

99
00:03:44,824 --> 00:03:46,226
-Up to 75 kg?
-I'm jealous.

100
00:03:46,292 --> 00:03:48,595
I wouldn't ask for anything more
if I were 75 kg.

101
00:03:48,661 --> 00:03:50,830
When I make music,
I always end up losing weight.

102
00:03:50,897 --> 00:03:53,333
-Let's make music.
-We should make music too.

103
00:03:53,399 --> 00:03:56,102
-Because your sleep schedule gets flipped?
-That's the biggest reason.

104
00:03:56,536 --> 00:03:57,837
JEALOUS(?)

105
00:03:57,904 --> 00:03:59,539
When I work late at night,

106
00:03:59,606 --> 00:04:01,841
-I only end up drinking stuff.
-Yes, it's too late to eat.

107
00:04:01,908 --> 00:04:03,910
So I did hear
there's a bigger ratio of thin people

108
00:04:03,977 --> 00:04:06,045
-among musicians.
-Is that so?

109
00:04:06,112 --> 00:04:08,915
That's the exact opposite for comedians.

110
00:04:08,982 --> 00:04:11,184
We need to stuff our faces with food
to get ideas.

111
00:04:11,251 --> 00:04:13,019
-That's how you get your brain going.
-Yes.

112
00:04:13,086 --> 00:04:14,487
Don't you need to eat to think?

113
00:04:14,554 --> 00:04:17,557
Chef Nam-no, you've gained weight
since you started cooking.

114
00:04:17,624 --> 00:04:20,526
-I wasn't this big either.
-We've seen your old picture.

115
00:04:20,593 --> 00:04:21,894
I was thin too.

116
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:24,430
-Yes, the Lee Seung-gi lookalike.
-How much did you weigh?

117
00:04:24,497 --> 00:04:26,366
I was 60 kg in high school.

118
00:04:26,432 --> 00:04:28,134
-What?
-You two were the same.

119
00:04:28,201 --> 00:04:31,137
-You were thin in high school.
-I gained weight after I started cooking.

120
00:04:31,204 --> 00:04:34,474
And at times,
when I cook for the customers,

121
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:35,408
the food looks so good.

122
00:04:35,475 --> 00:04:37,910
-And he eats later.
-I hold myself back at work,

123
00:04:37,977 --> 00:04:40,246
-…then I binge after my shift.
-I see.

124
00:04:40,313 --> 00:04:42,582
Chef Eddie, did you gain weight

125
00:04:42,649 --> 00:04:44,417
-after you started cooking?
-Right.

126
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:45,918
After I married.

127
00:04:46,152 --> 00:04:47,487
MARRIED MEN CAN RELATE

128
00:04:47,553 --> 00:04:50,156
We have a photo of him
from his younger days.

129
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:51,924
AH-HA

130
00:04:51,991 --> 00:04:54,827
-We've seen pictures of other chefs.
-He gained weight after marrying.

131
00:04:54,894 --> 00:04:56,262
AMAZED

132
00:04:56,329 --> 00:04:57,697
Look at you.

133
00:04:57,764 --> 00:04:59,699
He reminds me of Son Suk-ku.

134
00:04:59,766 --> 00:05:01,100
HAS THAT SON SUK-KU LOOK

135
00:05:01,634 --> 00:05:03,069
He has that neckline.

136
00:05:03,136 --> 00:05:05,772
Something about him seems wild.

137
00:05:05,838 --> 00:05:07,040
How old were you?

138
00:05:07,106 --> 00:05:09,942
It was when I opened
my first restaurant in New York.

139
00:05:10,009 --> 00:05:11,244
-I see.
-Really?

140
00:05:11,311 --> 00:05:13,313
-I was 25.
-You were 25.

141
00:05:13,379 --> 00:05:14,847
-First restaurant?
-Isn't that young?

142
00:05:14,914 --> 00:05:16,149
-It is.
-It's young.

143
00:05:16,215 --> 00:05:17,583
Some people start as a rookie at 25.

144
00:05:17,650 --> 00:05:19,452
I had lots of girlfriends back then.

145
00:05:19,519 --> 00:05:21,421
HEY

146
00:05:21,688 --> 00:05:23,022
HEHE

147
00:05:23,089 --> 00:05:25,258
That's the one thing I do remember.

148
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:27,193
We have another picture to share.

149
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:28,394
-What?
-Whose picture?

150
00:05:28,461 --> 00:05:31,030
A picture of COKUN after he bulked up.

151
00:05:31,097 --> 00:05:32,198
-I want to see it.
-Really?

152
00:05:32,265 --> 00:05:34,233
-Did you take your top off?
-Is your top off?

153
00:05:34,801 --> 00:05:36,102
Here's COKUN's picture.

154
00:05:37,337 --> 00:05:39,005
TOUGH GUY COKUN

155
00:05:39,372 --> 00:05:42,075
-Look at his back.
-You have a nice back.

156
00:05:42,141 --> 00:05:44,377
-That's insane. Look at his lats.
-Hey.

157
00:05:44,444 --> 00:05:45,912
-Are you working?
-He has a nice frame.

158
00:05:45,978 --> 00:05:48,314
I really was just… I…

159
00:05:48,381 --> 00:05:49,982
Why am I stuttering?

160
00:05:50,049 --> 00:05:52,618
-Spill the truth.
-I can't use the AC at the studio.

161
00:05:52,685 --> 00:05:53,820
You can't use the AC?

162
00:05:53,886 --> 00:05:55,555
-Because of the sound?
-It gets picked up.

163
00:05:56,889 --> 00:05:59,992
When I work with my friends,
we all have our tops off.

164
00:06:00,059 --> 00:06:01,494
PERSUADED

165
00:06:01,561 --> 00:06:03,996
-But you didn't used to, right?
-The bottom two are before.

166
00:06:04,063 --> 00:06:05,565
<i>-I knew it.</i>
<i>-These are before pictures?</i>

167
00:06:05,631 --> 00:06:07,667
-About a decade ago.
-Before you bulked up?

168
00:06:07,734 --> 00:06:10,370
Your arm probably shook like crazy
when you held your cat.

169
00:06:10,636 --> 00:06:11,571
HE GOT ME(?)

170
00:06:12,805 --> 00:06:13,973
Look at him taking a picture.

171
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:16,709
-Even the cat seems anxious.
-Yes.

172
00:06:16,776 --> 00:06:20,680
-It's looking for a place to land on.
-It's afraid it might fall.

173
00:06:20,747 --> 00:06:23,616
<i>-The before and after are different.</i>
<i>-They are.</i>

174
00:06:23,683 --> 00:06:25,451
-He's like an athlete.
-The thickness changed.

175
00:06:25,518 --> 00:06:28,688
-I can see more confidence in you.
-You've become much bigger.

176
00:06:28,755 --> 00:06:31,124
Are there any inconveniences
after you bulked up?

177
00:06:31,190 --> 00:06:33,326
Nothing inconvenient,
but I'm a bit bummed out.

178
00:06:33,393 --> 00:06:35,628
-Why?
-Why are you bummed out? You're healthier.

179
00:06:35,695 --> 00:06:38,998
-When I was 60 kg,
-He gained ten kg.

180
00:06:39,065 --> 00:06:41,801
-…pull-ups weren't hard.
-You were lighter.

181
00:06:41,868 --> 00:06:43,403
But I can't do that many now.

182
00:06:43,469 --> 00:06:45,204
How many can you do now?

183
00:06:45,271 --> 00:06:46,239
Five to six.

184
00:06:46,305 --> 00:06:47,874
-That's good.
-That's pretty good.

185
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:50,009
-Can you do pull-ups?
-Two?

186
00:06:50,076 --> 00:06:51,010
That's a lot.

187
00:06:51,077 --> 00:06:52,278
He's good.

188
00:06:52,345 --> 00:06:54,547
Let's ask Chef Nam-no too.

189
00:06:54,614 --> 00:06:56,215
What's harder for you?

190
00:06:56,282 --> 00:06:57,683
Brushing teeth.

191
00:06:57,750 --> 00:06:59,452
-Brushing teeth? What about it?
-Brushing teeth?

192
00:06:59,519 --> 00:07:01,788
When I drop toothpaste,
it lands on my tummy.

193
00:07:02,688 --> 00:07:03,790
It does. I know.

194
00:07:03,856 --> 00:07:06,159
At least you can save it.

195
00:07:06,225 --> 00:07:07,927
He can save it.

196
00:07:07,994 --> 00:07:10,096
-He can scoop it up.
-I can do this.

197
00:07:11,164 --> 00:07:12,932
-That's good.
-It's even better.

198
00:07:12,999 --> 00:07:15,268
-He can save it.
-That's a good thing.

199
00:07:15,334 --> 00:07:17,703
When I put socks on,

200
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:19,672
my tummy gets in the way.

201
00:07:19,739 --> 00:07:21,441
They're hard to put on.

202
00:07:21,507 --> 00:07:24,377
At times, I get cramps
when I take too long.

203
00:07:25,311 --> 00:07:26,512
I've been there.

204
00:07:26,579 --> 00:07:28,948
That's really painful.

205
00:07:29,015 --> 00:07:29,916
This part right here.

206
00:07:29,982 --> 00:07:32,351
I once hung out at a mountain stream,

207
00:07:32,418 --> 00:07:33,953
and I was on a tube.

208
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:36,022
And only my stomach
stuck out of the water,

209
00:07:36,088 --> 00:07:37,290
so a crane came and sat on it.

210
00:07:37,857 --> 00:07:39,392
A GREAT SPOT APPROVED BY A CRANE(?)

211
00:07:39,826 --> 00:07:41,227
FAINTING(?)

212
00:07:41,294 --> 00:07:43,963
-A crane?
-It must've thought I was an island,

213
00:07:44,030 --> 00:07:45,498
so it sat, and then flew off.

214
00:07:45,565 --> 00:07:48,234
-Sure.
-Might as well say a turtle sat there too.

215
00:07:48,301 --> 00:07:50,670
GOOD IDEA

216
00:07:50,736 --> 00:07:52,038
He's the biggest comic right now.

217
00:07:52,104 --> 00:07:55,675
COKUN is also an expert on coffee.

218
00:07:55,741 --> 00:07:57,276
COFFEE EXPERT

219
00:07:57,343 --> 00:07:59,145
He's a certified barista

220
00:07:59,212 --> 00:08:01,380
who also has
the sensory skills certification.

221
00:08:01,948 --> 00:08:04,350
I know what a barista is,
but sensory skills certification?

222
00:08:04,417 --> 00:08:05,718
It's the skill

223
00:08:05,785 --> 00:08:09,856
of writing down
the characteristics and taste

224
00:08:09,922 --> 00:08:14,427
of different coffee beans
after tasting them.

225
00:08:14,494 --> 00:08:16,629
You learn to differentiate
the characteristics

226
00:08:16,696 --> 00:08:18,397
and evaluate the grade.

227
00:08:18,631 --> 00:08:20,166
WOW

228
00:08:20,233 --> 00:08:22,969
Sung-jun, are you a certified barista?

229
00:08:23,035 --> 00:08:24,303
-I am.
-He ran a coffee shop.

230
00:08:24,370 --> 00:08:26,539
I had a coffee shop.
I'm a former coffee shop owner.

231
00:08:26,606 --> 00:08:28,040
What about the sensory skills?

232
00:08:28,107 --> 00:08:29,041
I don't have that one.

233
00:08:29,108 --> 00:08:32,645
That's a certification
for those who are really into coffee.

234
00:08:32,712 --> 00:08:34,747
It requires a bit more expertise.

235
00:08:34,814 --> 00:08:37,416
So you weren't really into coffee, then.

236
00:08:37,483 --> 00:08:38,784
And I had to close it down.

237
00:08:39,519 --> 00:08:42,121
-You're so cool about it.
-An espresso bar.

238
00:08:42,188 --> 00:08:43,890
He's a guy who's serious about coffee.

239
00:08:43,956 --> 00:08:45,825
We're about to look through his fridge,

240
00:08:45,892 --> 00:08:47,894
and it's rather decent-sized.

241
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:50,596
-It's much bigger than Woo-jae's fridge.
-Yes.

242
00:08:50,663 --> 00:08:52,098
-So that's a relief.
-He's right.

243
00:08:52,164 --> 00:08:54,667
And we hear that you have two fridges.

244
00:08:54,734 --> 00:08:56,168
-I have two fridges.
-Is it true?

245
00:08:56,235 --> 00:08:57,503
-That's unexpected.
-Really?

246
00:08:57,570 --> 00:08:59,005
One of them was built-in,

247
00:08:59,071 --> 00:09:03,009
and my brother went abroad to study,
so I brought his fridge home.

248
00:09:03,943 --> 00:09:05,044
That's why I have two.

249
00:09:05,111 --> 00:09:07,346
-I'm confident about my fridge.
-Are you?

250
00:09:07,413 --> 00:09:08,981
He feels confident.

251
00:09:09,048 --> 00:09:10,216
So you're better than Woo-jae?

252
00:09:10,283 --> 00:09:11,684
He can't compare to me.

253
00:09:11,751 --> 00:09:13,119
-He can't compare?
-What a relief.

254
00:09:13,185 --> 00:09:14,687
I've already studied the show.

255
00:09:14,754 --> 00:09:17,690
-He feels confident. Right?
-We haven't seen anyone this confident.

256
00:09:17,757 --> 00:09:19,659
-But he's a light eater.
-Yes.

257
00:09:19,725 --> 00:09:21,527
-You might be surprised.
-Really?

258
00:09:21,594 --> 00:09:24,230
-We present CODE KUNST's fridge!
-Fridge!

259
00:09:26,465 --> 00:09:28,401
MURMURING

260
00:09:28,467 --> 00:09:29,502
-…fridge!
-Fridge!

261
00:09:31,571 --> 00:09:33,105
AMAZED

262
00:09:33,372 --> 00:09:35,041
WIDE-EYED

263
00:09:35,107 --> 00:09:36,442
You're all surprised, right?

264
00:09:36,509 --> 00:09:39,312
-He has side dishes.
-Check the date. I didn't stage this.

265
00:09:39,378 --> 00:09:41,614
-This is pretty decent.
-It is.

266
00:09:41,681 --> 00:09:44,317
-I rarely get food delivered.
-I see.

267
00:09:44,383 --> 00:09:47,620
-Do you always cook for yourself?
-Yes, but I'm a bad cook,

268
00:09:47,687 --> 00:09:49,956
so I usually eat ready-made products.

269
00:09:50,022 --> 00:09:51,324
MOST DON'T REQUIRE LOTS OF WORK

270
00:09:51,390 --> 00:09:54,260
-The top. Look at these.
-He said he eats meat, and he has a lot.

271
00:09:54,327 --> 00:09:57,163
-And these are in the fridge.
-Which means he uses them quickly.

272
00:09:57,229 --> 00:09:58,965
-Yes, ribeye.
-That's a lot.

273
00:09:59,031 --> 00:10:01,133
Ribeye.

274
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,669
-Those last me a week.
-A week?

275
00:10:03,736 --> 00:10:05,171
-Wait a second.
-That's for one meal.

276
00:10:05,237 --> 00:10:08,674
-He said he eats half a pack at a time.
-Half a pack.

277
00:10:08,741 --> 00:10:10,676
-How big is this?
-How many grams?

278
00:10:10,743 --> 00:10:12,578
-It's 200 g.
-You eat 100 g per meal?

279
00:10:12,645 --> 00:10:14,046
-You only eat 100 g?
-He's eating

280
00:10:14,113 --> 00:10:16,616
as much as my five-year-old son.

281
00:10:16,682 --> 00:10:18,451
-My cat eats 100 g.
-Every morning.

282
00:10:18,517 --> 00:10:20,252
-Every morning?
-For a single meal?

283
00:10:20,319 --> 00:10:24,557
He was proud of eating that much meat.
I don't think he knows

284
00:10:24,624 --> 00:10:26,425
-how much others eat.
-I'm very proud of myself.

285
00:10:26,492 --> 00:10:28,694
-You bulked up after eating 100 g of meat?
-Wait.

286
00:10:28,761 --> 00:10:31,530
-That, protein shakes, and chicken breast.
-He eats protein.

287
00:10:31,831 --> 00:10:33,966
HOW DEPLORABLE

288
00:10:34,033 --> 00:10:37,169
-That's one bite for big eaters.
-One bite.

289
00:10:37,236 --> 00:10:40,106
Restaurant owners will get mad
if you try to order just 100 g.

290
00:10:40,172 --> 00:10:41,507
AN EMBARRASSING AMOUNT

291
00:10:41,574 --> 00:10:44,443
Well, when I walk into a restaurant,

292
00:10:44,510 --> 00:10:47,913
I don't feel particularly welcome
by the owners.

293
00:10:47,980 --> 00:10:49,515
They seem kind of unsure.

294
00:10:49,582 --> 00:10:51,417
Could you feel it?

295
00:10:51,484 --> 00:10:53,919
Sometimes they ask for an autograph,

296
00:10:53,986 --> 00:10:55,855
but I'm not sure if I'll be any help.

297
00:10:55,921 --> 00:10:57,056
WILL IT HELP THEIR BUSINESS?

298
00:10:57,123 --> 00:10:58,124
You question it while you sign it?

299
00:10:58,190 --> 00:11:02,228
People will think, "How much
could COKUN have eaten anyway?"

300
00:11:02,294 --> 00:11:05,865
Anyway, I see just ribeye here,
so do you have a favorite cut?

301
00:11:05,931 --> 00:11:10,269
I turn on the grocery shopping app once
every two to three days in the morning,

302
00:11:10,336 --> 00:11:13,039
and I usually buy the meat
shown at the very top.

303
00:11:13,105 --> 00:11:14,640
-I don't know any better.
-It could be…

304
00:11:14,707 --> 00:11:16,542
-…different each time, then.
-Yes.

305
00:11:16,609 --> 00:11:18,344
At times, I get pork shoulder,

306
00:11:18,411 --> 00:11:21,047
-and eye of round? That too.
-Wait, what?

307
00:11:21,113 --> 00:11:23,549
-That's the wrong cut.
-It's used for <i>yukhoe</i>.

308
00:11:23,616 --> 00:11:24,850
It was really thin.

309
00:11:25,951 --> 00:11:27,119
Chef Nam-no.

310
00:11:27,186 --> 00:11:29,088
He picks out his meat like this.

311
00:11:29,155 --> 00:11:31,924
-Yes?
-Give him some tips on buying meat.

312
00:11:31,991 --> 00:11:33,459
-I'm dying to know that.
-He wants to know.

313
00:11:33,526 --> 00:11:35,227
When you feel like something light,

314
00:11:35,294 --> 00:11:38,731
-get thinly sliced pork belly or brisket.
-They're heavy.

315
00:11:38,798 --> 00:11:41,901
-There's beef brisket?
-Yes, beef brisket.

316
00:11:41,967 --> 00:11:43,202
So beef has brisket?

317
00:11:43,269 --> 00:11:44,970
-He's…
-Brisket is usually beef.

318
00:11:45,037 --> 00:11:48,307
You need to specify
whether it's beef or pork.

319
00:11:48,374 --> 00:11:50,910
There's beef brisket,
and thinly sliced pork belly.

320
00:11:50,976 --> 00:11:52,878
-I understand now.
-That's pork.

321
00:11:52,945 --> 00:11:55,781
Excuse me. You do know the difference
between pork and beef, right?

322
00:11:55,848 --> 00:11:58,317
-My sense of taste is fine.
-Now.

323
00:11:58,384 --> 00:12:01,187
If you're planning to eat a lot,
start with the shoulder.

324
00:12:01,253 --> 00:12:02,855
-Pork shoulder.
-Pork shoulder.

325
00:12:02,922 --> 00:12:04,857
-I have to tell him that.
-Tell him accurately.

326
00:12:04,924 --> 00:12:06,492
Move on to pork belly,

327
00:12:06,559 --> 00:12:08,561
-then finish with pork neck.
-Pork neck.

328
00:12:08,627 --> 00:12:10,196
-That's a nice course.
-And some <i>jjigae.</i>

329
00:12:10,262 --> 00:12:12,865
-So pork neck is the last one?
-It has to be.

330
00:12:12,932 --> 00:12:14,133
Why is that?

331
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:15,968
-It's the fattiest cut.
-It's the fattiest.

332
00:12:16,035 --> 00:12:18,771
Because you can't eat as much
if you start with that?

333
00:12:18,838 --> 00:12:22,341
After that, have an order
of fried rice to eat a perfect meal.

334
00:12:22,408 --> 00:12:24,076
-You've probably…
-It calms your stomach.

335
00:12:24,143 --> 00:12:26,545
…never ordered rice to finish your meal
at a barbecue place.

336
00:12:26,612 --> 00:12:28,948
-I always do, actually.
-Do you get fried rice?

337
00:12:29,014 --> 00:12:32,284
People like going to eat with me.

338
00:12:32,351 --> 00:12:33,853
-They must like it.
-Of course.

339
00:12:33,919 --> 00:12:35,988
-It'd be great.
-They'll get to eat more.

340
00:12:36,055 --> 00:12:38,691
-It's the best.
-I offer all the best parts.

341
00:12:38,758 --> 00:12:40,726
-I usually do.
-That's the best.

342
00:12:40,793 --> 00:12:44,897
-COKUN, you're truly the best.
-It'd be perfect if you ate with him.

343
00:12:44,964 --> 00:12:46,832
There's something people never worry about

344
00:12:46,899 --> 00:12:48,167
-when they eat with me.
-What?

345
00:12:48,234 --> 00:12:51,303
About which <i>naengmyeon </i>to order,
between <i>mul </i>and <i>bibim-naengmyeon.</i>

346
00:12:51,370 --> 00:12:53,072
-Why not?
-I'll order one type,

347
00:12:53,139 --> 00:12:55,107
they'll order another,
and they'll get to try both.

348
00:12:55,174 --> 00:12:56,509
THEY'RE BOTH MINE!

349
00:12:56,575 --> 00:12:58,577
-They'll end up eating both.
-That's great.

350
00:12:58,644 --> 00:13:01,046
-That's pretty nice.
-He's a great guy to eat with.

351
00:13:01,113 --> 00:13:02,848
-People actually like that.
-I see.

352
00:13:02,915 --> 00:13:04,350
And I'm not a picky eater.

353
00:13:04,416 --> 00:13:05,651
You aren't picky?

354
00:13:05,718 --> 00:13:07,820
-What about spicy food?
-I like that too.

355
00:13:07,887 --> 00:13:09,288
I even like cilantro.

356
00:13:09,355 --> 00:13:11,090
-Do you enjoy spices?
-I do.

357
00:13:11,157 --> 00:13:12,691
-I just eat a little.
-He only eats a little.

358
00:13:12,758 --> 00:13:15,194
-You eat a little, but you enjoy them all.
-I do.

359
00:13:15,261 --> 00:13:17,930
What do you usually eat
for lunch and dinner?

360
00:13:17,997 --> 00:13:19,565
I often eat Vietnamese spring rolls.

361
00:13:19,632 --> 00:13:21,000
-I see.
-Vietnamese spring rolls?

362
00:13:21,066 --> 00:13:22,902
You'll probably see some rice paper there.

363
00:13:22,968 --> 00:13:26,172
-Here it is.
-Here it is. Rice paper.

364
00:13:26,238 --> 00:13:28,240
How many do you eat at a time?

365
00:13:28,307 --> 00:13:31,210
I think I eat between five and seven.

366
00:13:31,277 --> 00:13:32,645
-Not bad.
-For a meal?

367
00:13:32,711 --> 00:13:36,949
-So half of that meat goes into…
-You put it into the rolls?

368
00:13:37,016 --> 00:13:41,053
Excuse me. You split up
100 g of beef between seven spring rolls?

369
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:43,289
-So 15 g per roll?
-That's true.

370
00:13:43,355 --> 00:13:47,560
-You're making me frustrated.
-But thinner slices are better for rolls.

371
00:13:47,626 --> 00:13:51,897
-You only need one thin slice like this?
-A thin slice?

372
00:13:51,964 --> 00:13:54,366
-Do you include any veggies?
-I have veggies.

373
00:13:54,433 --> 00:13:56,702
-Vegetables.
-He has them in the crisper drawer.

374
00:13:56,769 --> 00:13:59,205
Bell peppers, lettuce, and cucumbers.

375
00:13:59,271 --> 00:14:02,007
I add those and some pineapples.

376
00:14:02,074 --> 00:14:04,043
-Pineapples are great.
-I see. Pineapples.

377
00:14:04,109 --> 00:14:06,312
-He has apples, pineapple, and plums.
-I do.

378
00:14:06,378 --> 00:14:08,714
What's your favorite fruit?

379
00:14:08,781 --> 00:14:10,549
This may make people furious,

380
00:14:10,616 --> 00:14:13,552
but I personally enjoy
warm and grilled fruit.

381
00:14:14,887 --> 00:14:16,155
-Instead of eating it cold?
-No…

382
00:14:16,222 --> 00:14:18,390
-I do like it cold too.
-Pineapple pizza?

383
00:14:18,958 --> 00:14:20,392
What?

384
00:14:20,459 --> 00:14:22,761
-Many people…
-I'm just asking.

385
00:14:22,828 --> 00:14:23,963
Many people dislike that,

386
00:14:24,029 --> 00:14:27,433
but I really love Hawaiian pizza.

387
00:14:27,499 --> 00:14:30,402
-With grilled and hot pineapple.
-Gosh. I don't like that.

388
00:14:30,469 --> 00:14:32,304
But I do draw a line somewhere.

389
00:14:32,371 --> 00:14:33,672
-Yes?
-Here's one.

390
00:14:33,739 --> 00:14:36,008
I often see people grill bananas,

391
00:14:36,075 --> 00:14:37,977
-but I didn't like that.
-People do that often.

392
00:14:38,744 --> 00:14:41,547
It feels like I'm grilling milk.

393
00:14:41,614 --> 00:14:44,483
HE HAS STRONG LIKES AND DISLIKES

394
00:14:44,550 --> 00:14:46,585
Wait, you have canned food,

395
00:14:46,652 --> 00:14:50,222
rice paper, and salt in here.

396
00:14:50,289 --> 00:14:52,358
Don't they usually belong on shelves?

397
00:14:52,424 --> 00:14:53,525
-You're right.
-That's true.

398
00:14:53,592 --> 00:14:56,495
-But they're all in your fridge.
-Yes.

399
00:14:56,562 --> 00:14:58,564
Well, I do not use my fridge

400
00:14:58,631 --> 00:15:01,834
only to keep food refrigerated.

401
00:15:01,901 --> 00:15:03,202
What?

402
00:15:03,269 --> 00:15:05,604
If I have space left,
I can use it as a drawer.

403
00:15:05,671 --> 00:15:07,473
-A drawer?
-A drawer?

404
00:15:07,973 --> 00:15:08,974
A CHILLED DRAWER

405
00:15:09,041 --> 00:15:10,509
A chilled drawer.

406
00:15:10,576 --> 00:15:14,179
-When I see the ingredients outside,
-Well, it's true, but…

407
00:15:14,246 --> 00:15:16,448
-…I find it uneasy.
-Because it looks messy?

408
00:15:16,515 --> 00:15:18,817
-I like to keep everything inside.
-Tucked away.

409
00:15:18,884 --> 00:15:20,619
-I see.
-Look at these.

410
00:15:20,686 --> 00:15:22,554
These are not usually kept in the fridge.

411
00:15:22,621 --> 00:15:24,223
-They're shelf-stable.
-He's right.

412
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:26,425
It looks nice when it's full.

413
00:15:28,527 --> 00:15:30,562
THE SECRET TO THE BULKY FRIDGE(?)

414
00:15:31,397 --> 00:15:32,531
Wait a second.

415
00:15:32,598 --> 00:15:35,334
-There is an actual drawer.
-It's there.

416
00:15:35,401 --> 00:15:36,435
-What?
-In the fridge.

417
00:15:36,502 --> 00:15:37,703
It's a drawer.

418
00:15:37,770 --> 00:15:39,238
These are funny.

419
00:15:39,305 --> 00:15:40,806
I see some medicine.

420
00:15:40,873 --> 00:15:44,176
-Why do you keep the tiger balm here?
-Seriously.

421
00:15:44,243 --> 00:15:46,779
-Tiger balm?
-Yes, it'll stink up the fridge.

422
00:15:46,845 --> 00:15:48,814
That must've been the medicine zone.

423
00:15:48,881 --> 00:15:49,782
-I guess.
-"Medicine zone."

424
00:15:49,848 --> 00:15:51,617
-For disinfection.
-This smells strong.

425
00:15:52,651 --> 00:15:54,086
-That's…
-What's this?

426
00:15:54,153 --> 00:15:55,187
-A hair band?
-What is it?

427
00:15:55,254 --> 00:15:57,756
-A key chain? What is it?
-A key chain?

428
00:15:57,823 --> 00:16:00,292
-Don't you have places for it outside?
-That's a key chain.

429
00:16:00,359 --> 00:16:04,096
-I wanted to get rid of it from my sight.
-I see.

430
00:16:04,163 --> 00:16:05,164
-I see.
-Right.

431
00:16:05,230 --> 00:16:08,133
-Why is this in here?
-A tape measure?

432
00:16:08,200 --> 00:16:09,935
-A tape measure?
-He has a tape measure in here.

433
00:16:10,002 --> 00:16:11,303
-A tape measure? Why?
-How odd.

434
00:16:11,370 --> 00:16:12,604
He has a tape measure in here.

435
00:16:12,671 --> 00:16:15,007
I couldn't find it.
So that's where it was.

436
00:16:15,074 --> 00:16:16,709
FINDING LOST ARTICLES

437
00:16:16,775 --> 00:16:19,211
That drawer used to be outside,

438
00:16:19,278 --> 00:16:20,646
and I just stuck the whole thing in there.

439
00:16:22,047 --> 00:16:23,749
USING HIS FRIDGE 200%

440
00:16:23,816 --> 00:16:26,385
The packets on top
are orange-flavored sugar.

441
00:16:26,452 --> 00:16:29,555
-It's sugar?
-I sometimes use that to make coffee.

442
00:16:30,622 --> 00:16:31,924
Why is this in here?

443
00:16:31,991 --> 00:16:33,926
Because I had space?

444
00:16:35,227 --> 00:16:37,196
The stir-fried squid he mentioned is here.

445
00:16:37,262 --> 00:16:40,933
He thought we'd leave a container behind,
but we took all three.

446
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,701
That's my favorite side dish.

447
00:16:42,768 --> 00:16:43,936
-That sounds good.
-Squid.

448
00:16:44,003 --> 00:16:46,472
You've stocked up on these.
Are you working on this one?

449
00:16:46,538 --> 00:16:50,209
That was why I split them into three.
I thought they'd leave one.

450
00:16:52,211 --> 00:16:54,580
-They took them all.
-So you felt flustered.

451
00:16:54,646 --> 00:16:56,382
Wait a second.

452
00:16:56,448 --> 00:16:58,183
Is this quince <i>cheong?</i>

453
00:16:58,250 --> 00:17:00,652
-Yes, it's some kind of <i>cheong.</i>
-Something's written here.

454
00:17:01,353 --> 00:17:02,421
"From Ho-jin"?

455
00:17:02,488 --> 00:17:03,522
It's from Ho-jin.

456
00:17:03,589 --> 00:17:04,823
-Girlfriend?
-Ho-jin?

457
00:17:04,890 --> 00:17:07,159
It says he's in class 4-3.

458
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:10,429
-Class 4-3? Do they mean 43 years old?
-That one

459
00:17:10,496 --> 00:17:13,232
is from my mother's friend's son.

460
00:17:13,298 --> 00:17:16,835
He made that himself
and gave it to me as a present.

461
00:17:16,902 --> 00:17:19,071
It's been three years,
but I can still use it, right?

462
00:17:19,138 --> 00:17:20,639
-I've never opened it.
-That's fine.

463
00:17:20,706 --> 00:17:22,207
-That's as good as new.
-Yes.

464
00:17:23,675 --> 00:17:24,676
-Is it new?
-It is.

465
00:17:24,743 --> 00:17:27,146
-You can eat <i>cheong</i> for a decade, right?
-It's <i>cheong.</i>

466
00:17:27,212 --> 00:17:28,680
-Even a century.
-If it's sealed.

467
00:17:29,048 --> 00:17:30,816
HIS SELF-PROCLAIMED EXPIRATION DATE
IS ON ANOTHER LEVEL

468
00:17:30,883 --> 00:17:34,019
-I see a box in here too.
-What is it?

469
00:17:34,086 --> 00:17:36,622
-It's…
-That's the only ingredient

470
00:17:36,688 --> 00:17:39,391
I bought for this occasion.

471
00:17:39,458 --> 00:17:40,359
-You did?
-What is it?

472
00:17:40,426 --> 00:17:41,927
It's my favorite food,

473
00:17:41,994 --> 00:17:44,897
-What is it?
-…but I can't cook it at home.

474
00:17:44,963 --> 00:17:46,698
I tried once and failed miserably.

475
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:49,468
-Stir-fried squid?
-Is it the squid?

476
00:17:49,535 --> 00:17:51,203
-Stir-fried squid…
-You talked about it a lot.

477
00:17:51,270 --> 00:17:52,738
-No.
-You said it was your favorite.

478
00:17:52,805 --> 00:17:54,139
Let me open it.

479
00:17:54,206 --> 00:17:55,974
He bought this ingredient.

480
00:17:56,041 --> 00:17:58,110
-Blue crabs.
-Crabs.

481
00:17:58,177 --> 00:17:59,378
-They're great.
-Looks delicious.

482
00:17:59,445 --> 00:18:00,546
-Shrimp.
-Yes.

483
00:18:00,612 --> 00:18:02,448
-They're in season now.
-Blue crabs.

484
00:18:02,514 --> 00:18:06,718
-Do you like these?
-I grew up in Incheon,

485
00:18:06,785 --> 00:18:08,454
so I had crabs very often.

486
00:18:08,520 --> 00:18:10,656
I think I had them every weekend.

487
00:18:10,722 --> 00:18:14,993
But no matter how much I craved it,
I couldn't cook it myself.

488
00:18:15,594 --> 00:18:18,464
I looked up recipes online and tried that,

489
00:18:18,530 --> 00:18:19,731
-What did…
-…and it turned out horrendous.

490
00:18:19,798 --> 00:18:21,733
Which crab dish did you try to make?

491
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:23,902
It was red braised crabs, I think.

492
00:18:23,969 --> 00:18:25,070
-Braised crabs?
-Braised dish?

493
00:18:25,137 --> 00:18:28,307
Maybe it wasn't braised.
Maybe <i>tang,</i> a soup, but less soupy.

494
00:18:28,373 --> 00:18:31,577
-A crab braise-<i>tang.</i>
-They mostly turn out delicious.

495
00:18:31,643 --> 00:18:33,779
-A braised dish, but with a little soup?
-It's like this.

496
00:18:33,846 --> 00:18:36,415
-It's braised with some soup.
-Like braised seafood.

497
00:18:36,482 --> 00:18:38,450
-Isn't that <i>beombeok?</i>
<i>-Beombeok.</i>

498
00:18:38,517 --> 00:18:42,788
-Yes. It's reddish. My parents made that.
-It's this reddish dish.

499
00:18:42,855 --> 00:18:44,656
It's like braised seafood
mainly with crabs.

500
00:18:44,723 --> 00:18:46,692
-It's closer to a braised dish.
-Yes, a braised dish.

501
00:18:46,758 --> 00:18:48,760
I felt really bad for the crabs,

502
00:18:48,827 --> 00:18:50,162
-and it was a waste of money.
-For the crabs?

503
00:18:50,229 --> 00:18:51,130
We're sorry, crabs.

504
00:18:51,196 --> 00:18:53,398
I felt like everything was a waste,
including my time.

505
00:18:53,465 --> 00:18:56,668
-It must've felt that way.
-But video recipes are easy to follow.

506
00:18:56,735 --> 00:18:58,137
-Yes.
-So wouldn't it have been fine

507
00:18:58,203 --> 00:18:59,438
if you followed it to a T?

508
00:18:59,505 --> 00:19:03,108
-It's because I get creative while I cook.
-While cooking?

509
00:19:03,175 --> 00:19:04,610
-He's an artist.
-Instead of following it?

510
00:19:04,676 --> 00:19:05,811
-He does art.
-What did you do?

511
00:19:05,878 --> 00:19:07,179
The recipe would list

512
00:19:07,246 --> 00:19:11,216
the ingredients like lemon juice,
cooking wine, sugar, and more.

513
00:19:11,283 --> 00:19:13,952
But there's always one or two
I don't have at home.

514
00:19:14,019 --> 00:19:15,487
-It happens.
-That's why.

515
00:19:15,554 --> 00:19:17,189
So I end up thinking it'd be okay

516
00:19:17,256 --> 00:19:20,792
-to replace those with something similar.
-Understandable.

517
00:19:20,859 --> 00:19:22,794
If I don't have Cheongyang peppers,

518
00:19:22,861 --> 00:19:24,263
-Then?
-…then I use bell peppers.

519
00:19:24,329 --> 00:19:27,266
-They're way too different.
-They look similar.

520
00:19:27,332 --> 00:19:29,301
-They're both peppers.
-Aren't they like friends?

521
00:19:29,368 --> 00:19:31,470
They look like
Cheongyang pepper's friends.

522
00:19:32,104 --> 00:19:33,338
But they aren't close.

523
00:19:33,405 --> 00:19:35,140
How creative were you
with the braised crab?

524
00:19:35,207 --> 00:19:37,342
That was probably
when I used bell peppers.

525
00:19:37,409 --> 00:19:39,611
-For braised crab?
-They couldn't have worked.

526
00:19:39,678 --> 00:19:41,113
It could've tasted Mexican.

527
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:42,514
TURNS INTO MEXICAN BRAISED CRAB

528
00:19:42,581 --> 00:19:46,151
This is the thing.
You have to use all those crabs today.

529
00:19:46,218 --> 00:19:47,886
-He can't cook them.
-No leftovers.

530
00:19:47,953 --> 00:19:49,821
Otherwise, they'll stay inside
for five years.

531
00:19:49,888 --> 00:19:51,089
WILL GET LOCKED UP FOR 5 YEARS(?)

532
00:19:51,156 --> 00:19:53,659
-These three shrimp were on the house?
-Yes.

533
00:19:53,725 --> 00:19:55,160
-They were on the house.
-On the house.

534
00:19:55,227 --> 00:19:56,395
I see. All right.

535
00:19:56,461 --> 00:19:59,565
-There's sauce.
-He must've cooked with these.

536
00:19:59,631 --> 00:20:03,569
-Yes, I bought all of them together.
-At once?

537
00:20:03,635 --> 00:20:07,372
It looks like he got these in June 2025.

538
00:20:07,439 --> 00:20:10,342
-Yes.
-I must've tried to cook in 2025.

539
00:20:10,409 --> 00:20:13,445
He bought all these
in an attempt to cook in 2025.

540
00:20:13,512 --> 00:20:16,215
-I probably got them on the same day.
-The same day.

541
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:19,851
-They're the same class.
-They've expired a year ago.

542
00:20:20,419 --> 00:20:21,453
-They're still fine.
-Yes.

543
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:22,854
STILL FRESH(?)

544
00:20:22,921 --> 00:20:25,624
-He has some meat in the freezer.
-Meat.

545
00:20:25,691 --> 00:20:26,992
Some pork.

546
00:20:27,726 --> 00:20:30,495
-Chicken breast.
-Thigh steak. Probably to bulk up.

547
00:20:30,562 --> 00:20:34,366
I get sick of chicken breast,
so I usually stick to thigh meat.

548
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:38,170
And some bread.
You haven't had any of these.

549
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:40,372
They're always a problem.

550
00:20:40,439 --> 00:20:43,041
These are all new.
They got stashed in here.

551
00:20:43,875 --> 00:20:45,177
They're loafing around in there.

552
00:20:45,244 --> 00:20:49,314
-Why are they all in the freezer?
-The breads are loafing around.

553
00:20:49,381 --> 00:20:52,317
When you order bread,
you have to order a certain amount, right?

554
00:20:52,384 --> 00:20:55,954
So I usually throw everything in there
except for the ones I want now.

555
00:20:56,021 --> 00:20:57,823
-People do this often.
-They're fine.

556
00:20:57,889 --> 00:20:58,957
Look, coffee.

557
00:20:59,024 --> 00:21:00,959
-You're right.
-Coffee.

558
00:21:01,026 --> 00:21:03,528
-I have to store them outside.
-Yes.

559
00:21:03,595 --> 00:21:05,597
So you don't keep these in the fridge?

560
00:21:05,664 --> 00:21:07,132
He knows that much.

561
00:21:07,199 --> 00:21:10,636
-Do you brew your own coffee?
-Yes, about 90% of the time.

562
00:21:10,702 --> 00:21:13,639
<i>I'm asking because</i>
<i>I don't know much about coffee,</i>

563
00:21:13,705 --> 00:21:15,240
but does it have recipes?

564
00:21:15,307 --> 00:21:17,342
You can adjust the settings

565
00:21:17,409 --> 00:21:19,611
for the grind size you want.

566
00:21:19,678 --> 00:21:22,481
Then, it's about the speed
at which you pour,

567
00:21:22,547 --> 00:21:24,049
the flow.

568
00:21:24,116 --> 00:21:28,887
I have to time it right,
and the temperature needs to be right.

569
00:21:28,954 --> 00:21:32,057
So the recipe makes
a pleasant cup of coffee.

570
00:21:32,124 --> 00:21:33,425
-He should open a coffee shop.
-I see.

571
00:21:33,492 --> 00:21:34,726
-How cool.
-A coffee shop?

572
00:21:34,793 --> 00:21:38,997
Says a guy who can't even tell
beef brisket and pork belly apart.

573
00:21:39,064 --> 00:21:42,501
-When it comes to coffee…
-He's a coffee expert.

574
00:21:42,934 --> 00:21:44,369
FINISHED CHECKING OUT COKUN'S FRIDGE

575
00:21:44,436 --> 00:21:46,805
We're done looking through
CODE KUNST's fridge.

576
00:21:46,872 --> 00:21:49,574
What does he want to eat?
Show us his orders.

577
00:21:49,641 --> 00:21:51,076
COKUN's orders.

578
00:21:51,143 --> 00:21:51,977
-Show Me the Crab.
-Crab.

579
00:21:52,044 --> 00:21:54,313
-Show Me the Crab?
-Show Me the Crab.

580
00:21:54,379 --> 00:21:56,982
I want to eat a crab dish,

581
00:21:57,049 --> 00:21:58,116
and I don't care what it is.

582
00:21:58,183 --> 00:21:59,951
-I see.
-Because I have

583
00:22:00,018 --> 00:22:02,788
memories of eating
such an awful crab dish,

584
00:22:02,854 --> 00:22:05,023
I want to turn it around this time.

585
00:22:05,090 --> 00:22:06,491
It's soul food to me.

586
00:22:06,558 --> 00:22:08,727
What's the second order?

587
00:22:08,794 --> 00:22:11,563
-Why Can't We Stop Warm Fruit?
-Warm Fruit.

588
00:22:12,130 --> 00:22:13,932
-Oh, warm fruit?
-Yes.

589
00:22:13,999 --> 00:22:16,668
He's a guy who enjoys warm pineapples.

590
00:22:16,735 --> 00:22:18,170
-Tell us about it.
-Warm fruit?

591
00:22:18,236 --> 00:22:22,007
I got curious
because I love to eat fruit warm.

592
00:22:22,074 --> 00:22:25,010
I wanted to know
if there were other ways to eat it

593
00:22:25,077 --> 00:22:29,247
other than grilling pineapples
like I usually do.

594
00:22:29,314 --> 00:22:32,317
The name of your order
sounds similar to a sitcom.

595
00:22:32,384 --> 00:22:34,920
-Yes.
-Just like <i>Why Can't We Stop Them.</i>

596
00:22:34,986 --> 00:22:38,590
I've watched every single episode
of <i>Why Can't We Stop Them</i>

597
00:22:38,657 --> 00:22:39,858
over seven times.

598
00:22:40,726 --> 00:22:42,194
-He must love it.
-Seven times?

599
00:22:42,260 --> 00:22:43,729
The one featuring Shin Gu.

600
00:22:43,795 --> 00:22:45,063
-Right?
-Yes.

601
00:22:45,130 --> 00:22:49,601
-He must like it a lot.
-He used his favorite sitcom.

602
00:22:49,668 --> 00:22:51,269
It also featured Noh Joo-hyun.

603
00:22:51,336 --> 00:22:52,504
It does.

604
00:22:52,571 --> 00:22:55,140
Why Can't We Stop Warm Fruit?

605
00:22:55,207 --> 00:22:58,710
PRODUCTION SUPPORT
BY ALTER EGO: NOH JOO-HYUN

606
00:22:58,777 --> 00:23:01,380
We have four people left.

607
00:23:01,446 --> 00:23:04,750
Son Jong-won, Jung Ho-young,
Kim Poong, and Kwon Sung-jun.

608
00:23:04,816 --> 00:23:07,219
I'll go for Show Me the Crab.

609
00:23:07,486 --> 00:23:08,987
WOW

610
00:23:09,054 --> 00:23:11,757
My favorite ingredient is also shellfish,

611
00:23:11,823 --> 00:23:13,892
and I also have good memories of crabs.

612
00:23:13,959 --> 00:23:15,193
So I'll take that one.

613
00:23:15,260 --> 00:23:17,462
What are good memories of crab?

614
00:23:17,529 --> 00:23:19,631
I'll share it later while cooking.

615
00:23:19,698 --> 00:23:22,067
-I have a story.
-People like that should join.

616
00:23:22,134 --> 00:23:23,168
Yes, Poong?

617
00:23:23,235 --> 00:23:25,537
I also have a memory about crabs…

618
00:23:25,604 --> 00:23:26,638
Do you have something?

619
00:23:26,705 --> 00:23:29,474
…and a connection with them.

620
00:23:29,541 --> 00:23:33,311
So I'll also jump into Show Me the Crab.

621
00:23:33,378 --> 00:23:34,713
Have you cooked with crab?

622
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:36,681
Sure, a few times, I think.

623
00:23:36,748 --> 00:23:38,784
-Yes.
-With Poong's decision,

624
00:23:38,850 --> 00:23:41,086
the other two are automatically placed.

625
00:23:41,153 --> 00:23:45,056
-Ho-young and Jong-won will go to fruit.
-Warm fruit.

626
00:23:45,123 --> 00:23:47,426
Why didn't you jump in?
You make good crab dishes.

627
00:23:47,492 --> 00:23:50,796
I was thinking
about what to make with crabs,

628
00:23:50,862 --> 00:23:52,831
-then he jumped in first.
-I see.

629
00:23:52,898 --> 00:23:54,266
I can't cook with fruit.

630
00:23:54,332 --> 00:23:55,167
Is that okay, Jong-won?

631
00:23:55,233 --> 00:23:56,668
I like warm food, anyway.

632
00:23:56,735 --> 00:23:57,903
A warm guy.

633
00:23:57,969 --> 00:23:59,171
-I see.
-The WG.

634
00:23:59,237 --> 00:24:03,275
Then we'll begin
Sung-jun and Poong's round first!

635
00:24:05,944 --> 00:24:10,315
<i>We're about to begin the first cook-off</i>
<i>with CODE KUNST's ingredients.</i>

636
00:24:10,382 --> 00:24:13,585
The show's hyena,
Webtoon Artist Kim Poong,

637
00:24:13,652 --> 00:24:17,789
versus the show's anteater,
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

638
00:24:17,856 --> 00:24:19,858
Chef Sung-jun said something like this.

639
00:24:19,925 --> 00:24:23,228
"Today, Poong is at his worst."

640
00:24:23,295 --> 00:24:24,196
-Precisely.
-What?

641
00:24:24,262 --> 00:24:26,398
"I'll practically get a star for free."

642
00:24:26,465 --> 00:24:28,500
The thing is, at the very least,

643
00:24:28,567 --> 00:24:31,436
he always managed to destroy Nam-no.

644
00:24:31,503 --> 00:24:34,906
But after his loss
in the previous episode,

645
00:24:34,973 --> 00:24:38,176
I believe he's a bit groggy.
One nudge is enough to knock him out.

646
00:24:38,910 --> 00:24:41,847
He probably suffered
from huge psychological damage.

647
00:24:41,913 --> 00:24:44,916
So today, I'll win
as long as I serve my food.

648
00:24:45,217 --> 00:24:46,985
SWAG

649
00:24:47,052 --> 00:24:47,986
As long as he finishes.

650
00:24:48,053 --> 00:24:50,222
-Why does he talk like that?
-There's a flow.

651
00:24:50,288 --> 00:24:52,057
Everyone, you didn't know?

652
00:24:52,123 --> 00:24:55,026
Chef Nam-no is also an amazing chef.

653
00:24:55,093 --> 00:24:56,828
-Yes, that's true.
-He is.

654
00:24:56,895 --> 00:25:00,365
-He took 4th place on <i>CCW 1.</i>
-Yes.

655
00:25:00,432 --> 00:25:02,234
It wasn't that big of a deal for me

656
00:25:02,300 --> 00:25:04,636
to lose against a great chef
because I'm a webtoon artist.

657
00:25:04,703 --> 00:25:07,272
-Why would it have an impact on me?
-That's true.

658
00:25:07,339 --> 00:25:10,041
-He's chill about it.
-Keep the fight there. Don't drag me in.

659
00:25:11,142 --> 00:25:13,178
Anyway, you're about to cook
with blue crabs.

660
00:25:13,245 --> 00:25:18,750
And they've already told us
that they had lots of connections.

661
00:25:18,817 --> 00:25:20,619
COKUN.

662
00:25:20,685 --> 00:25:23,221
-You're from Incheon, right?
-I am.

663
00:25:23,288 --> 00:25:25,690
My wife's family lives in Incheon.

664
00:25:25,757 --> 00:25:27,526
-Is that so?
-Yes.

665
00:25:27,592 --> 00:25:30,529
-Gosh.
-You somehow got Incheon involved.

666
00:25:30,595 --> 00:25:32,531
And my wife's family
is as good as my family.

667
00:25:32,597 --> 00:25:34,165
-Yes, of course.
-Yes.

668
00:25:34,232 --> 00:25:38,169
I always eat things like blue crab <i>tang,</i>
seasoned blue crabs,

669
00:25:38,236 --> 00:25:40,472
and <i>gejang</i> in both soy sauce
and chili sauce.

670
00:25:40,539 --> 00:25:41,706
It's a crabload of crabs.

671
00:25:43,341 --> 00:25:47,045
That's how much I love crabs,

672
00:25:47,112 --> 00:25:49,347
and I have a strong tie with them.

673
00:25:49,414 --> 00:25:51,082
He must feel like a hometown buddy.

674
00:25:51,149 --> 00:25:54,886
We're pretty much distant relatives.

675
00:25:54,953 --> 00:25:57,522
Sung-jun, what's your story
about blue crabs?

676
00:25:57,589 --> 00:25:59,791
Well, my dear crabs

677
00:26:00,525 --> 00:26:03,929
are the ingredients
who led me to this place today.

678
00:26:03,995 --> 00:26:05,430
-Really? When?
-How so?

679
00:26:05,497 --> 00:26:06,932
About three years ago,

680
00:26:06,998 --> 00:26:09,167
it was the semifinal of <i>CCW.</i>

681
00:26:09,234 --> 00:26:10,435
The semifinal.

682
00:26:10,502 --> 00:26:13,872
-I used crabs and made <i>gegukji,</i>
-What?

683
00:26:13,939 --> 00:26:16,107
-Right.
-…and it helped me win.

684
00:26:16,174 --> 00:26:17,208
Was that the case?

685
00:26:17,275 --> 00:26:18,710
Yes, I went straight to the finals.

686
00:26:18,777 --> 00:26:20,579
-I see.
-Wait.

687
00:26:21,112 --> 00:26:23,214
You can already tell by your responses

688
00:26:23,281 --> 00:26:24,583
that no one remembers.

689
00:26:24,649 --> 00:26:26,051
Did that really happen?

690
00:26:26,117 --> 00:26:27,519
We didn't know it was that.

691
00:26:27,586 --> 00:26:31,022
We only remember Chef Edward!

692
00:26:31,089 --> 00:26:33,224
-I thought he was the winner.
-Wasn't he?

693
00:26:33,291 --> 00:26:36,928
What we remember is
Chef Edward eating tofu like this.

694
00:26:36,995 --> 00:26:40,398
Many people hoped Chef Edward would win,

695
00:26:40,465 --> 00:26:43,335
-but this random guy won.
-Right?

696
00:26:43,401 --> 00:26:44,636
CHUCKLE

697
00:26:44,703 --> 00:26:46,638
Chef Sam just laughed a bit too loudly.

698
00:26:46,705 --> 00:26:50,208
I feel so bad for him. He was the winner.

699
00:26:50,275 --> 00:26:53,545
Whenever we tease Chef Sung-jun,

700
00:26:53,612 --> 00:26:54,613
Sam always laughs a lot.

701
00:26:54,879 --> 00:26:56,281
HE DOES ENJOY IT THE MOST

702
00:26:56,348 --> 00:26:57,816
He always enjoys it the most.

703
00:26:57,882 --> 00:27:01,586
-He likes it a bit too much.
-Anyway, you made such an amazing dish.

704
00:27:01,653 --> 00:27:03,521
-Sure.
-All right.

705
00:27:03,588 --> 00:27:06,024
-Great job.
-We didn't know.

706
00:27:06,858 --> 00:27:08,893
CHEF SUNG-JUN'S INGREDIENTS:
BLUE CRAB, WHITELEG SHRIMP, BELL PEPPER

707
00:27:08,960 --> 00:27:10,996
SMOKED BONELESS THIGH, TOMATO,
STEAMED CORN, VICTORY ONION, AND MORE

708
00:27:11,062 --> 00:27:13,264
Chef Sung-jun, what are you making?

709
00:27:13,331 --> 00:27:15,533
The dish I'm making today is called

710
00:27:16,101 --> 00:27:17,936
Crab W.

711
00:27:18,003 --> 00:27:19,771
-Crab W.
-Crab W.

712
00:27:19,838 --> 00:27:22,107
-Crab W. What's it like?
-Crab W.

713
00:27:22,173 --> 00:27:24,142
I was inspired by a crab boil.

714
00:27:24,209 --> 00:27:26,244
-A crab boil?
-A crab boil?

715
00:27:26,311 --> 00:27:30,982
It's a dish rooted in the American South,
made with seafood, crab, shrimp, and corn,

716
00:27:31,049 --> 00:27:32,684
seasoned with a bit of spice.

717
00:27:32,751 --> 00:27:36,087
It's usually thrown into a bag,
shaken, and then served.

718
00:27:36,154 --> 00:27:37,555
I know what that is.

719
00:27:37,622 --> 00:27:39,891
-And you eat it by hand,
-Yes.

720
00:27:39,958 --> 00:27:41,126
…but I'll make it neat for you

721
00:27:41,192 --> 00:27:43,361
-and let you eat the bag too.
-The bag?

722
00:27:43,428 --> 00:27:44,829
He can eat the bag too?

723
00:27:44,896 --> 00:27:47,732
You said you liked
Vietnamese spring rolls,

724
00:27:47,799 --> 00:27:49,934
so I'll make the bag with rice paper,

725
00:27:50,001 --> 00:27:53,605
stuff it with ingredients,
and let you eat it with the rice paper.

726
00:27:53,672 --> 00:27:55,874
-I'll make that crab boil.
-That's amazing.

727
00:27:55,940 --> 00:27:59,277
-Great idea.
-To add more crab flavor,

728
00:27:59,344 --> 00:28:01,913
I'll serve it with some bisque sauce too.

729
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:04,416
-Will you remove the shell?
-Yes, everything.

730
00:28:04,482 --> 00:28:06,751
-It'll be very easy to eat.
-Easy to eat.

731
00:28:06,818 --> 00:28:08,286
That's a big Crab W.

732
00:28:08,353 --> 00:28:10,221
-It is a big W.
-It's a W in many ways.

733
00:28:10,288 --> 00:28:11,423
-Yes, it's a big W.
-Yes.

734
00:28:11,489 --> 00:28:12,757
Crab W.

735
00:28:13,591 --> 00:28:15,627
WEBTOON ARTIST KIM POONG'S INGREDIENTS:
BLUE CRAB, BELL PEPPER, TOMATO

736
00:28:15,694 --> 00:28:17,762
CURRY CUBE, GREEK YOGURT, MILK,
CREAM CHEESE, OYSTER SAUCE, SEASONED SALT

737
00:28:17,829 --> 00:28:20,632
Up against him is Poong.
What are you making?

738
00:28:20,699 --> 00:28:23,234
The name of my dish is

739
00:28:23,301 --> 00:28:26,337
Pu Phat Poong Curry, COKUN Khrap.

740
00:28:26,404 --> 00:28:28,907
-Pu Phat Poong Curry, COKUN Khrap.
-Whoa.

741
00:28:28,973 --> 00:28:30,175
-Nice name.
-Pu Phat Poong Curry.

742
00:28:30,241 --> 00:28:32,510
Pu Phat Poong Curry, COKUN Khrap.

743
00:28:32,577 --> 00:28:37,482
Pu phat phong curry is
one of the more famous crab dishes.

744
00:28:37,549 --> 00:28:41,553
I'll add my twist with some <i>doenjang</i>

745
00:28:41,619 --> 00:28:46,391
and make a curry dish
with a hint of Korea.

746
00:28:46,458 --> 00:28:47,959
You have a bad memory

747
00:28:48,026 --> 00:28:50,395
-of crab and bell peppers mix.
-I do.

748
00:28:50,462 --> 00:28:52,630
I'll change that bad memory

749
00:28:52,697 --> 00:28:55,800
-and update it into a good one.
-Into a good memory?

750
00:28:55,867 --> 00:28:58,536
I'd like to turn it into a good memory.

751
00:28:58,603 --> 00:29:00,305
He'll change my memories with his dish.

752
00:29:00,371 --> 00:29:01,873
-Right?
-Wow.

753
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:04,342
-"<i>Khop khun khrap</i>" means thank you, right?
-Yes.

754
00:29:04,409 --> 00:29:06,544
I thought I'd easily win,

755
00:29:06,611 --> 00:29:08,580
so I'd like to say that in advance.

756
00:29:08,646 --> 00:29:09,514
COKUN Khrap.

757
00:29:09,581 --> 00:29:11,783
-Goodness.
-All right, then.

758
00:29:11,850 --> 00:29:14,319
Let us begin the cook-off.

759
00:29:14,953 --> 00:29:16,988
Here to take Poong down and win a star,

760
00:29:17,055 --> 00:29:19,524
looking forward to a big Crab W today,

761
00:29:19,591 --> 00:29:21,226
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

762
00:29:21,292 --> 00:29:22,127
Crab W.

763
00:29:22,193 --> 00:29:26,765
But this guy, Kim Poong, says,
when Sung-jun gets smug,

764
00:29:26,831 --> 00:29:28,767
he's always a big L.

765
00:29:29,467 --> 00:29:32,370
Who'll be the winner of Show Me the Crab?

766
00:29:32,437 --> 00:29:36,074
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

767
00:29:36,141 --> 00:29:37,375
-Yeah!
-Good luck!

768
00:29:37,442 --> 00:29:38,676
"Yeah"?

769
00:29:38,743 --> 00:29:39,944
Aw, gosh. Yeah.

770
00:29:40,011 --> 00:29:42,614
I haven't heard
that kind of "yeah" in so long.

771
00:29:42,680 --> 00:29:44,716
I've never hosted a hip-hop thing.

772
00:29:44,783 --> 00:29:45,683
Yeah.

773
00:29:45,750 --> 00:29:47,852
…begin!

774
00:29:48,319 --> 00:29:49,988
HOW WILL THE WINNER OF CCW START?

775
00:29:50,054 --> 00:29:51,022
Hah!

776
00:29:51,289 --> 00:29:53,491
1. SALT WATER AND BOIL BLUE CRABS
WITH WHISKEY AND BEER

777
00:29:53,558 --> 00:29:54,759
-Beer.
-He's using…

778
00:29:54,826 --> 00:29:56,561
-Yes, and beer.
-…whiskey and beer.

779
00:29:56,628 --> 00:29:57,762
SLAMMING

780
00:29:57,829 --> 00:29:58,663
It'll be delicious.

781
00:29:58,730 --> 00:30:00,799
-Whoa.
-He's destroying the crabs.

782
00:30:00,865 --> 00:30:03,935
-Are you using just the meat?
-No, the shell too.

783
00:30:04,002 --> 00:30:05,937
-Wait, what?
-I know. To make bisque.

784
00:30:06,004 --> 00:30:07,839
It's for the sauce. The bisque sauce.

785
00:30:07,906 --> 00:30:10,275
Sung-jun has already begun to cook.

786
00:30:11,276 --> 00:30:13,545
That sauce is going to be rich.

787
00:30:13,611 --> 00:30:15,180
SHELLFISH, MUSTER

788
00:30:15,880 --> 00:30:18,449
POONG STARTS WITH INSTANT RICE!

789
00:30:18,983 --> 00:30:21,019
Poong starts by prepping blue crabs.

790
00:30:21,085 --> 00:30:23,054
Open the blue crab…

791
00:30:23,121 --> 00:30:23,955
This crab…

792
00:30:24,022 --> 00:30:26,090
Why can't I remove the belly meat?

793
00:30:26,157 --> 00:30:28,026
I have a feeling he has only seen it once.

794
00:30:28,092 --> 00:30:30,528
-You can use your hand.
-You're using a knife?

795
00:30:30,595 --> 00:30:33,231
-You can open it with your hands.
-Does he know how to do it?

796
00:30:33,298 --> 00:30:35,333
-It's sloppy.
-I don't think he's done it before.

797
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,069
Poong preps blue crabs.

798
00:30:38,136 --> 00:30:41,739
I'm wondering if he had ever touched

799
00:30:41,806 --> 00:30:43,708
a crab before in his life.

800
00:30:43,775 --> 00:30:44,876
I saw this on the Internet.

801
00:30:44,943 --> 00:30:46,411
He said he saw it on the Internet.

802
00:30:46,477 --> 00:30:48,313
He's doing it
just as he saw it on the Internet.

803
00:30:48,613 --> 00:30:49,781
THE REAL HIP-HOP SPIRIT

804
00:30:49,848 --> 00:30:52,050
You can use scissors to cut them off.

805
00:30:52,116 --> 00:30:54,319
-People usually use them.
-I did that too.

806
00:30:54,385 --> 00:30:56,955
I think I was a bit better than Poong.

807
00:30:57,822 --> 00:30:59,290
THEY'RE ABOUT THE SAME(?)

808
00:30:59,357 --> 00:31:01,960
-I see.
-I think he's scared of crabs.

809
00:31:02,026 --> 00:31:04,062
You're right. He doesn't look well.

810
00:31:04,128 --> 00:31:05,897
-He seems frozen.
-You seem cautious.

811
00:31:05,964 --> 00:31:08,533
I also watched a video three times.

812
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:11,369
I think Poong watched it just once.

813
00:31:12,170 --> 00:31:14,005
STOP THAT CRAB…

814
00:31:14,072 --> 00:31:17,008
-Whoa. Yes.
-Sung-jun chops very quickly.

815
00:31:18,009 --> 00:31:19,310
He's really fast.

816
00:31:19,377 --> 00:31:20,478
HIS SIGNATURE QUICK CHOPS

817
00:31:20,979 --> 00:31:22,413
MINCED GARLIC, GROUND PEPPER

818
00:31:22,480 --> 00:31:23,948
LOOK AT THAT SPEED

819
00:31:25,483 --> 00:31:26,918
The crabs turned red already.

820
00:31:27,352 --> 00:31:28,820
A DASH OF WHISKEY

821
00:31:30,121 --> 00:31:31,723
He made that fire look so cool.

822
00:31:31,789 --> 00:31:33,224
It's called <i>flambé.</i>

823
00:31:33,291 --> 00:31:35,693
-Removing the alcohol.
-Is that a technique?

824
00:31:35,760 --> 00:31:36,961
Yes, it is.

825
00:31:37,028 --> 00:31:38,630
You can see that he's experienced.

826
00:31:38,696 --> 00:31:40,765
-Sung-jun's already done.
-He is.

827
00:31:40,832 --> 00:31:43,301
It took him two minutes to prep crabs.

828
00:31:43,368 --> 00:31:44,335
Thirteen minutes left.

829
00:31:44,402 --> 00:31:46,271
Thirteen minutes. Darn it.

830
00:31:46,337 --> 00:31:48,907
-The speed difference is huge at first.
-Isn't it?

831
00:31:48,973 --> 00:31:51,409
-But Poong always delivers.
-Yes.

832
00:31:51,476 --> 00:31:53,778
-You'll be surprised.
-Right.

833
00:31:54,679 --> 00:31:56,147
Poong starts cooking too.

834
00:31:58,416 --> 00:31:59,617
Absolutely uneven.

835
00:31:59,684 --> 00:32:03,054
He claims it doesn't need to be even
if it'll get digested anyway.

836
00:32:03,621 --> 00:32:04,989
What about your chili oil?

837
00:32:05,056 --> 00:32:05,957
I'm not doing it today.

838
00:32:06,324 --> 00:32:07,725
WHAT'S GOING ON?

839
00:32:07,792 --> 00:32:08,693
What's going on?

840
00:32:08,760 --> 00:32:11,095
Is that just green onion oil?

841
00:32:11,162 --> 00:32:13,464
It's for the fried rice.

842
00:32:13,531 --> 00:32:14,899
The fried rice.

843
00:32:14,966 --> 00:32:16,701
The blue crabs look amazing.

844
00:32:16,768 --> 00:32:18,002
It's filled with meat.

845
00:32:18,069 --> 00:32:20,605
Poong's squeezing the meat out.

846
00:32:20,672 --> 00:32:22,407
It's hard to get the meat out
from a raw crab.

847
00:32:22,707 --> 00:32:23,741
NOT EASY TO GET MEAT

848
00:32:23,808 --> 00:32:25,810
-Should he cook it?
-He'll get the shells in it too.

849
00:32:25,877 --> 00:32:28,947
-What if he ends up chewing on shells?
-Yes.

850
00:32:29,814 --> 00:32:31,115
-Nothing came out.
-Nothing's there.

851
00:32:31,182 --> 00:32:32,183
You didn't get anything.

852
00:32:32,250 --> 00:32:34,218
Push it with a rolling pin.
You have to do that.

853
00:32:34,285 --> 00:32:36,788
-Yes, you should've done that.
-A stick? Yes.

854
00:32:37,956 --> 00:32:39,490
-That's the trick.
-He should've done that.

855
00:32:39,557 --> 00:32:40,925
It's still not a lot.

856
00:32:40,992 --> 00:32:43,494
-He should push harder.
-If it's fresher, it's harder.

857
00:32:43,561 --> 00:32:45,330
It's tough to use just the crab meat.

858
00:32:45,830 --> 00:32:47,165
TOUGH TO PUSH THE MEAT OUT

859
00:32:47,231 --> 00:32:49,400
-That's not so bad.
-Good one.

860
00:32:49,467 --> 00:32:51,135
It got stuck on the side of the pan.

861
00:32:51,202 --> 00:32:52,837
Don't waste it.

862
00:32:53,571 --> 00:32:56,874
You used two crabs
and only got that much meat?

863
00:32:57,208 --> 00:32:58,409
WON'T LISTEN, TO THE OVEN

864
00:32:58,543 --> 00:32:59,377
GRABS THE RICE

865
00:32:59,444 --> 00:33:00,645
He's making crab meat fried rice.

866
00:33:01,813 --> 00:33:03,681
It's literally crab meat and fried rice.

867
00:33:03,748 --> 00:33:05,583
Is it just egg fried rice?

868
00:33:05,650 --> 00:33:07,952
-That looks so ordinary.
-Doesn't it?

869
00:33:08,019 --> 00:33:12,290
Crab fried rice is usually
made with frozen meat.

870
00:33:12,357 --> 00:33:13,624
It's quite the luxury.

871
00:33:13,691 --> 00:33:17,061
-You haven't done that.
-No, not with fresh crab meat.

872
00:33:17,128 --> 00:33:20,798
I mean, if you're going to turn
those blue crabs into fried rice…

873
00:33:21,632 --> 00:33:24,702
Are you leaving the stems on the tomatoes?

874
00:33:24,769 --> 00:33:25,837
I don't have to.

875
00:33:25,903 --> 00:33:27,038
You should cut them off.

876
00:33:27,105 --> 00:33:28,573
2. BLEND TOMATOES

877
00:33:29,240 --> 00:33:30,241
WATER

878
00:33:30,308 --> 00:33:33,911
Sung-jun looks extra-relaxed today.

879
00:33:33,978 --> 00:33:35,747
-Yes, he seems quite relaxed.
-Yes.

880
00:33:35,813 --> 00:33:37,248
He said he headed

881
00:33:37,315 --> 00:33:40,118
straight to the finals on <i>CCW</i>
thanks to his crab dish.

882
00:33:40,184 --> 00:33:42,220
You're pouring that in there?

883
00:33:42,286 --> 00:33:43,921
-That's so pink.
-I wonder what it's like.

884
00:33:43,988 --> 00:33:46,190
-Tomato adds a lot of depth.
-I see.

885
00:33:46,257 --> 00:33:47,658
So he's making broth with tomatoes.

886
00:33:47,725 --> 00:33:48,893
FOR THE BISQUE SAUCE!

887
00:33:48,960 --> 00:33:50,928
He said he'd let him eat the bag too,

888
00:33:50,995 --> 00:33:52,497
so what about the shell?

889
00:33:52,997 --> 00:33:55,466
-The color already looks delicious.
-It does.

890
00:33:55,767 --> 00:33:57,468
3. ADD MINCED GARLIC INTO OIL

891
00:33:58,036 --> 00:33:58,970
CRABS INTO THE OIL!

892
00:33:59,037 --> 00:34:00,705
Poong's having a rough time.

893
00:34:00,772 --> 00:34:01,706
He's frying the crabs.

894
00:34:01,773 --> 00:34:03,741
He's making his busy face.

895
00:34:03,808 --> 00:34:06,144
BIG FROWN

896
00:34:06,711 --> 00:34:09,347
You can tell
that he's never cooked with crabs before.

897
00:34:09,414 --> 00:34:12,216
-He's all over the place.
-It's not working out.

898
00:34:12,417 --> 00:34:13,651
WHAT'S NEXT?

899
00:34:14,218 --> 00:34:15,319
Yogurt.

900
00:34:15,553 --> 00:34:17,121
MILK, COOKING WINE, EGG

901
00:34:17,188 --> 00:34:18,089
One more egg.

902
00:34:18,156 --> 00:34:19,857
Egg. One more egg.

903
00:34:19,924 --> 00:34:21,726
One more egg.

904
00:34:21,793 --> 00:34:22,627
EGGS

905
00:34:22,693 --> 00:34:23,594
CURRY BLOCK

906
00:34:23,661 --> 00:34:24,495
CREAM CHEESE

907
00:34:25,063 --> 00:34:26,497
Is he making a drink?

908
00:34:26,564 --> 00:34:28,900
He blended those
because there was no coconut milk.

909
00:34:28,966 --> 00:34:30,568
I see. That's sauce?

910
00:34:30,635 --> 00:34:33,037
He also said
it'd be like <i>doenjang-jjigae</i> too.

911
00:34:33,104 --> 00:34:35,506
-I added <i>doenjang</i> at the start.
-You did?

912
00:34:35,973 --> 00:34:38,009
SUNG-JUN CHOPS ANOTHER ONION!

913
00:34:38,076 --> 00:34:40,545
Chef Sung-jun is doing this and that.

914
00:34:40,611 --> 00:34:42,280
Are you making fried rice?

915
00:34:42,346 --> 00:34:44,382
These are ingredients for the boil.

916
00:34:44,449 --> 00:34:46,884
You said you'd let him eat the bag too.

917
00:34:46,951 --> 00:34:48,219
Let's see how it turns out.

918
00:34:48,286 --> 00:34:51,989
He'll probably make a huge piece
of rice paper and wrap the ingredients.

919
00:34:52,223 --> 00:34:53,391
NEXT, PREPPING RAW SHRIMP!

920
00:34:53,458 --> 00:34:55,426
He's peeling the shrimp quickly.

921
00:34:56,127 --> 00:34:57,995
He's trained.

922
00:34:58,062 --> 00:35:00,331
-That's so fast.
-How does he peel it so fast?

923
00:35:00,631 --> 00:35:01,499
CUTTING INTO BITE SIZES

924
00:35:01,566 --> 00:35:04,368
That's another ingredient
for the rice paper wrap.

925
00:35:04,435 --> 00:35:06,571
That filling looks great, Sung-jun.

926
00:35:06,637 --> 00:35:08,005
It's made with chicken and shrimp.

927
00:35:08,072 --> 00:35:09,640
-Everything.
-I see.

928
00:35:09,707 --> 00:35:12,310
He only used the meat.

929
00:35:12,376 --> 00:35:14,345
If Sung-jun wins this round,

930
00:35:14,412 --> 00:35:16,948
he'll be in a tie with Poong
with ten wins.

931
00:35:17,682 --> 00:35:21,686
Heal the memories of bad bell peppers
with his bell peppers.

932
00:35:21,986 --> 00:35:23,354
4. FILL BELL PEPPER WITH CRAB FRIED RICE

933
00:35:23,421 --> 00:35:25,223
-He's filling the bell pepper with rice.
-Rice?

934
00:35:25,556 --> 00:35:27,925
HE'S USING BELL PEPPER AS A BOWL?

935
00:35:27,992 --> 00:35:30,061
-The crab fried rice?
-I see.

936
00:35:31,429 --> 00:35:33,131
It's probably still hot.

937
00:35:34,332 --> 00:35:35,466
He's enduring it.

938
00:35:38,569 --> 00:35:39,737
Let's go.

939
00:35:40,138 --> 00:35:42,406
POONG SAN

940
00:35:42,473 --> 00:35:44,742
MOVE ASIDE, CHOI SAN

941
00:35:44,809 --> 00:35:46,444
-He's staying calm!
-It's hot!

942
00:35:46,511 --> 00:35:47,912
It's Poong San!

943
00:35:47,979 --> 00:35:49,947
-Poong San.
-It's still hot!

944
00:35:50,615 --> 00:35:51,949
HANG IN THERE, I'M POONG SAN…

945
00:35:52,517 --> 00:35:53,651
That bell pepper is raw.

946
00:35:53,718 --> 00:35:55,353
-Are you going to steam it?
-He'll have to grill it.

947
00:35:55,419 --> 00:35:56,721
-Will he cook it?
-He has to.

948
00:35:57,021 --> 00:35:59,190
COAT WITH STARCH AND COOK!

949
00:35:59,257 --> 00:36:01,692
-Ah-ha.
-That looks so good.

950
00:36:01,759 --> 00:36:04,929
And the bell pepper will give out
its sweetness too.

951
00:36:05,596 --> 00:36:07,131
He's putting them together.

952
00:36:07,198 --> 00:36:09,467
I guess he'll heal
the bad memory of bell peppers

953
00:36:09,534 --> 00:36:11,169
by using that bell pepper.

954
00:36:11,235 --> 00:36:12,637
BELL PEPPER FRIED RICE FOR YOUR SOUL

955
00:36:12,703 --> 00:36:15,706
BUT HE'S UP AGAINST THE WINNER OF CCW 1

956
00:36:16,007 --> 00:36:19,310
How's he going to make a bag
with rice paper?

957
00:36:20,278 --> 00:36:21,412
By putting them together.

958
00:36:21,479 --> 00:36:23,447
It's similar to Poong's plastic pants.

959
00:36:24,315 --> 00:36:25,650
It's not.

960
00:36:25,716 --> 00:36:27,485
-You're copying my recipe?
-No.

961
00:36:28,252 --> 00:36:29,353
Not at all.

962
00:36:29,420 --> 00:36:32,590
-Poong did that for JY Park.
-The plastic pants.

963
00:36:32,657 --> 00:36:34,892
Is this a copy?

964
00:36:34,959 --> 00:36:36,794
Really? I don't remember.

965
00:36:37,094 --> 00:36:39,664
COOKED CRABS, OUT

966
00:36:39,730 --> 00:36:41,065
He's very relaxed.

967
00:36:41,132 --> 00:36:42,500
