1215
00:45:44,274 --> 00:45:45,743
-That's the one for TV?
-You practiced that?

1216
00:45:45,809 --> 00:45:47,044
A BIT AWKWARD

1217
00:45:47,111 --> 00:45:49,613
<i>I don't think you need to practice that.</i>

1218
00:45:49,680 --> 00:45:51,782
That slight char makes it better.

1219
00:45:51,849 --> 00:45:53,917
A HUGE TWIST

1220
00:45:53,984 --> 00:45:56,653
At times, charred meat
tastes a bit better, right?

1221
00:45:56,720 --> 00:45:59,623
When it's slightly charred at the edge.
It tastes like that.

1222
00:45:59,690 --> 00:46:00,657
It adds flavor.

1223
00:46:00,724 --> 00:46:03,293
It looks like he's taking the Crab W.

1224
00:46:03,360 --> 00:46:04,828
-What now?
-Crab L.

1225
00:46:04,895 --> 00:46:07,364
It enhances the bell pepper's flavor.

1226
00:46:07,431 --> 00:46:09,800
It's often charred
when used as an ingredient.

1227
00:46:09,867 --> 00:46:11,702
-Yes, right.
-Right. That's true.

1228
00:46:13,003 --> 00:46:13,971
It wasn't intentional.

1229
00:46:14,037 --> 00:46:16,206
-No, it was.
-Was it really?

1230
00:46:16,273 --> 00:46:20,077
-It's charred and peeled, but he didn't.
-It's usually peeled. Yes.

1231
00:46:20,144 --> 00:46:22,179
-He was too busy.
-Wait.

1232
00:46:22,246 --> 00:46:23,347
Are you drooling?

1233
00:46:23,413 --> 00:46:25,282
I can see it on his face.

1234
00:46:25,349 --> 00:46:26,817
Do you have to take it home?

1235
00:46:28,085 --> 00:46:30,387
-He's made a lot.
-There's a lot.

1236
00:46:30,454 --> 00:46:32,823
-You can try it.
-I can't eat a lot anyway.

1237
00:46:32,890 --> 00:46:34,758
Anyway, it's so good.

1238
00:46:34,825 --> 00:46:36,794
He eats so well.

1239
00:46:37,594 --> 00:46:38,662
Wow.

1240
00:46:38,729 --> 00:46:41,799
-Is it that good?
-We saw the process ourselves, right?

1241
00:46:41,865 --> 00:46:42,800
It's unbelievable.

1242
00:46:42,866 --> 00:46:44,301
AN UNIMAGINABLE RESULT

1243
00:46:44,368 --> 00:46:47,171
I usually eat my food a little bland,

1244
00:46:47,237 --> 00:46:51,675
and I can't eat a lot of curry
if it smells too strong.

1245
00:46:51,742 --> 00:46:53,710
But this is just enough.

1246
00:46:53,777 --> 00:46:56,547
I neutralized the flavor
mostly with Greek yogurt.

1247
00:46:56,613 --> 00:46:58,248
Gosh. This is really good.

1248
00:46:58,315 --> 00:47:00,217
I've never seen you eat so well.

1249
00:47:00,284 --> 00:47:03,320
Are you holding him in check
in case he eats too much?

1250
00:47:03,387 --> 00:47:05,856
-Were you a bit anxious?
-Yes. I was anxious.

1251
00:47:05,923 --> 00:47:08,458
-I want to share it with you.
-Really? I'd love to.

1252
00:47:08,525 --> 00:47:11,528
Next, Chef Sung-jun's dish, Crab W.

1253
00:47:11,829 --> 00:47:14,498
A CRAB BOIL IN AN EDIBLE BAG

1254
00:47:15,299 --> 00:47:17,835
SERVED WITH RICH BISQUE SAUCE
MADE WITH BLUE CRAB

1255
00:47:18,836 --> 00:47:22,105
CHEF KWON SUNG-JUN'S CRAB W

1256
00:47:22,172 --> 00:47:25,209
-We'll put the dish over there.
-Okay.

1257
00:47:26,944 --> 00:47:28,745
HAPPY

1258
00:47:30,814 --> 00:47:31,982
That should be mine.

1259
00:47:32,349 --> 00:47:33,717
SORRY

1260
00:47:33,784 --> 00:47:34,918
-Okay.
-Crab W.

1261
00:47:34,985 --> 00:47:36,720
I can even eat the bag of this crab boil.

1262
00:47:37,554 --> 00:47:40,324
-Will it capture his appetite?
-That's…

1263
00:47:40,390 --> 00:47:42,092
AUTOMATIC SMILE

1264
00:47:42,926 --> 00:47:44,361
WHAT'S IT LIKE?

1265
00:47:45,696 --> 00:47:47,965
Can you taste Sung-jun's class?

1266
00:47:48,532 --> 00:47:49,366
SLURP

1267
00:47:49,800 --> 00:47:51,168
CONTINUES TO DRINK THE SOUP QUIETLY

1268
00:47:51,235 --> 00:47:54,071
That was a very delicious dish,
but this is more like

1269
00:47:54,137 --> 00:47:55,305
a culinary creation.

1270
00:47:55,572 --> 00:47:57,574
IT'S BETTER THAN FOOD(?)

1271
00:47:57,641 --> 00:48:00,911
-It's all in the aroma.
-And the flavor?

1272
00:48:00,978 --> 00:48:04,348
Next, open the bag
and eat everything as you like.

1273
00:48:04,414 --> 00:48:05,849
-A crab boil.
-Wow.

1274
00:48:05,916 --> 00:48:07,184
It's full of stuff.

1275
00:48:07,251 --> 00:48:10,487
<i>I added crab meat, shrimp, and corn too.</i>

1276
00:48:11,054 --> 00:48:13,056
-He added everything.
-It looks good.

1277
00:48:13,123 --> 00:48:16,660
-The southern dish.
-The American crab boil is tough to eat.

1278
00:48:16,727 --> 00:48:18,729
-And it's messy.
-It gets messy.

1279
00:48:18,795 --> 00:48:19,963
You can eat it neatly.

1280
00:48:20,030 --> 00:48:21,531
Will he like it?

1281
00:48:23,567 --> 00:48:26,270
He's knocked out again.

1282
00:48:26,336 --> 00:48:27,437
FULL OF CURIOSITY

1283
00:48:27,504 --> 00:48:29,640
-What's it like?
-I've figured it out.

1284
00:48:29,706 --> 00:48:33,677
-What?
-I thought I was bad at food reviews.

1285
00:48:33,744 --> 00:48:35,746
But I've just never had
anything really good.

1286
00:48:35,812 --> 00:48:39,082
You didn't feel the need
to prepare a good food review.

1287
00:48:39,149 --> 00:48:40,450
It comes out naturally.

1288
00:48:40,517 --> 00:48:44,788
Chicken and crab seem to go together
much better than I thought.

1289
00:48:44,855 --> 00:48:49,626
And I believe the chicken I had
was smoked,

1290
00:48:49,693 --> 00:48:52,296
and it made the filling smoky too.

1291
00:48:52,629 --> 00:48:54,031
IT'S SUCH A CRAB W

1292
00:48:54,097 --> 00:48:57,000
A crab boil is made
with smoked bell peppers,

1293
00:48:57,067 --> 00:48:57,901
but you didn't have it.

1294
00:48:57,968 --> 00:48:59,603
Then I realized
the chicken packets you had

1295
00:48:59,670 --> 00:49:03,140
were all made with the seasoning
from the American South.

1296
00:49:03,206 --> 00:49:04,474
Why does he seem smart today?

1297
00:49:04,541 --> 00:49:07,678
-It's the Cajun style.
-A smart chef, Kwon Sung-jun.

1298
00:49:07,744 --> 00:49:09,913
Also, crabs are the best
when someone else picks out

1299
00:49:09,980 --> 00:49:11,481
-just the meat for you.
-Just the meat.

1300
00:49:11,548 --> 00:49:13,150
-That was the key point.
-Just the meat.

1301
00:49:13,216 --> 00:49:14,851
-True.
-I did that for the fried rice too.

1302
00:49:15,185 --> 00:49:17,321
"JUST THE MEAT" BATTLE BEGINS

1303
00:49:17,387 --> 00:49:20,023
-You did?
-Of course. I can do it too.

1304
00:49:20,924 --> 00:49:23,760
I'm already thinking about this
as I'm eating.

1305
00:49:23,827 --> 00:49:25,062
I can't choose.

1306
00:49:25,128 --> 00:49:26,663
-I see.
-It must be a close fight.

1307
00:49:26,730 --> 00:49:29,700
I was more confused
as I continued to eat more.

1308
00:49:29,766 --> 00:49:31,201
It's so good.

1309
00:49:31,268 --> 00:49:33,770
It's really easy to eat
when it's just the meat.

1310
00:49:33,837 --> 00:49:36,239
-It's easy.
-That was his intention.

1311
00:49:36,306 --> 00:49:39,910
Anyway, we're also glad to see
COKUN, the light eater, eat so well.

1312
00:49:39,977 --> 00:49:41,812
-It's really good.
-He's eating a lot.

1313
00:49:41,878 --> 00:49:44,081
I guess I only ate bad food.

1314
00:49:44,381 --> 00:49:45,482
IT LOOKS SO GOOD

1315
00:49:45,549 --> 00:49:47,184
There's so much good food in the world.

1316
00:49:47,250 --> 00:49:49,152
The joy of eating is a huge part of life.

1317
00:49:49,219 --> 00:49:51,254
He's a guy who can eat with gusto.

1318
00:49:51,321 --> 00:49:53,390
He's different from Joo Woo-jae.

1319
00:49:53,457 --> 00:49:54,458
APPALLED!

1320
00:49:54,958 --> 00:49:56,994
-We're different.
-Yes, you are different.

1321
00:49:57,060 --> 00:49:59,663
He'll start looking for food
while making music

1322
00:49:59,730 --> 00:50:01,431
-late at night.
-He'll want to eat.

1323
00:50:01,498 --> 00:50:03,800
Maybe I'll return when I'm at 80 kg.

1324
00:50:04,201 --> 00:50:05,402
YOU'RE WELCOME ANYTIME

1325
00:50:05,469 --> 00:50:07,971
Does the rice paper go well with the dish?

1326
00:50:08,038 --> 00:50:09,006
The rice paper

1327
00:50:09,072 --> 00:50:13,410
slowly absorbs the soup over time

1328
00:50:13,477 --> 00:50:14,978
and becomes very soft.

1329
00:50:15,045 --> 00:50:16,813
-Gosh.
-He didn't need practice.

1330
00:50:16,880 --> 00:50:18,982
-All he had to do was eat good food.
-You…

1331
00:50:19,049 --> 00:50:20,617
-He's not listening at all.
-You…

1332
00:50:20,684 --> 00:50:23,220
-You just want to eat that, don't you?
-Yes, right now.

1333
00:50:23,286 --> 00:50:24,588
You should have some.

1334
00:50:24,654 --> 00:50:26,323
I want to see how you react.

1335
00:50:26,390 --> 00:50:28,859
-Poong's dish.
-You seemed very curious.

1336
00:50:28,925 --> 00:50:30,627
That's usually for Nam-no.

1337
00:50:30,694 --> 00:50:32,129
-It's mine.
-It's usually his.

1338
00:50:32,195 --> 00:50:33,363
Lee Seon-min.

1339
00:50:33,430 --> 00:50:34,498
FROWN

1340
00:50:34,564 --> 00:50:36,600
-It got his appetite going.
-It's really good.

1341
00:50:36,666 --> 00:50:38,335
Why are you glaring at him?

1342
00:50:38,402 --> 00:50:41,004
-It's that good.
-Yes?

1343
00:50:41,071 --> 00:50:43,206
I haven't had pu phat phong curry

1344
00:50:43,273 --> 00:50:44,708
too many times in my life,

1345
00:50:46,076 --> 00:50:47,477
but this is the best one.

1346
00:50:47,544 --> 00:50:48,812
-I'm telling you.
-Among everything?

1347
00:50:48,879 --> 00:50:50,447
-It's the best…
-The best?

1348
00:50:50,514 --> 00:50:52,249
The best pu phat phong curry of my life.

1349
00:50:52,582 --> 00:50:54,217
AN UNLIKELY ENCOUNTER

1350
00:50:54,284 --> 00:50:57,287
I've had it about 30 times,
and I didn't expect

1351
00:50:57,354 --> 00:50:58,822
to have the best one here.

1352
00:50:59,156 --> 00:51:01,224
MADE THE BEST ONE
OF THEIR LIVES IN 15 MINUTES

1353
00:51:01,291 --> 00:51:03,060
-Try the crab too.
-Yes.

1354
00:51:03,627 --> 00:51:05,562
THE HUMAN DISHWASHER IS BACK

1355
00:51:06,163 --> 00:51:07,130
He's like a thresher.

1356
00:51:07,998 --> 00:51:09,366
He's destroying the shells.

1357
00:51:09,433 --> 00:51:11,001
Mmm.

1358
00:51:11,468 --> 00:51:13,737
EVOLVING INTO A THRESHER

1359
00:51:14,137 --> 00:51:16,273
TENACIOUS

1360
00:51:16,339 --> 00:51:17,441
Is that Yoon Nam-no?

1361
00:51:18,842 --> 00:51:21,478
-He can really eat.
-At least he spits out the shell.

1362
00:51:21,812 --> 00:51:23,847
WAS WORRIED

1363
00:51:23,914 --> 00:51:26,116
-I should leave it out.
-You should.

1364
00:51:26,183 --> 00:51:27,250
It's really good.

1365
00:51:27,818 --> 00:51:28,652
-And the fried rice.
-Fried rice.

1366
00:51:30,787 --> 00:51:32,355
-Oh my gosh.
-Gosh.

1367
00:51:32,422 --> 00:51:33,457
He has a big mouth.

1368
00:51:33,523 --> 00:51:37,160
-He ate it all.
-It's too hot.

1369
00:51:37,227 --> 00:51:40,263
It's still hot?

1370
00:51:40,630 --> 00:51:41,932
THE MACHINE PAUSES BRIEFLY

1371
00:51:41,998 --> 00:51:43,867
You should've checked first.

1372
00:51:43,934 --> 00:51:45,836
But still, he doesn't spit it out.

1373
00:51:45,902 --> 00:51:48,738
-He can't.
-He doesn't spit it out.

1374
00:51:49,406 --> 00:51:51,341
Seon-min doesn't spit it out.

1375
00:51:51,408 --> 00:51:52,709
It's still hot.

1376
00:51:52,776 --> 00:51:54,744
When they first started cooking,

1377
00:51:54,811 --> 00:51:57,447
-I thought they had a big gap of skills.
-I see.

1378
00:51:57,514 --> 00:51:59,616
But in the end,
you proved it with the result.

1379
00:51:59,683 --> 00:52:01,852
I also agree with what you said.

1380
00:52:01,918 --> 00:52:03,920
The curry flavor isn't too strong,

1381
00:52:03,987 --> 00:52:05,555
<i>so I can enjoy it until my last bite.</i>

1382
00:52:05,622 --> 00:52:08,391
Nothing he does seems intentional,
but there are intentions.

1383
00:52:08,458 --> 00:52:10,127
-It's intentional.
-That's how he cooks.

1384
00:52:10,193 --> 00:52:13,697
He'll hand over the rest to Nam-no.

1385
00:52:14,197 --> 00:52:15,699
A WHOLESOME MOMENT
BETWEEN THE DISHWASHER DUO

1386
00:52:16,733 --> 00:52:18,568
-My feed.
-It goes around like this?

1387
00:52:18,635 --> 00:52:19,636
Thank you.

1388
00:52:19,936 --> 00:52:20,971
DYING TO KNOW

1389
00:52:21,037 --> 00:52:24,541
-It's a smaller version.
-Yes, a smaller portion.

1390
00:52:24,608 --> 00:52:26,943
Please try Chef Sung-jun's dish, Crab W.

1391
00:52:27,010 --> 00:52:28,445
He's going to drink that.

1392
00:52:28,512 --> 00:52:31,381
GRABS THE PLATE AND TRIES THE SAUCE FIRST

1393
00:52:31,448 --> 00:52:33,150
I should learn from him.

1394
00:52:34,017 --> 00:52:35,685
OH MY

1395
00:52:36,319 --> 00:52:37,154
Right?

1396
00:52:37,220 --> 00:52:39,623
Do you get what I mean
by culinary creation now?

1397
00:52:39,689 --> 00:52:41,424
It'll hit you hard.

1398
00:52:41,491 --> 00:52:43,393
It's my first time
trying this bisque sauce.

1399
00:52:44,127 --> 00:52:47,063
It feels like the ocean is in here.

1400
00:52:47,130 --> 00:52:48,865
-In one bite.
-A single bite.

1401
00:52:48,932 --> 00:52:50,400
GOES IN

1402
00:52:51,968 --> 00:52:52,936
Gosh.

1403
00:52:53,003 --> 00:52:55,105
-Isn't this round hard?
-Yes.

1404
00:52:55,172 --> 00:52:56,106
They're different.

1405
00:52:56,173 --> 00:52:59,242
-There's chicken and shrimp in there too.
-The dishwasher.

1406
00:52:59,309 --> 00:53:00,810
The dishwasher.

1407
00:53:00,877 --> 00:53:02,812
-He's cleaning it.
-The dishwasher.

1408
00:53:02,879 --> 00:53:05,048
-Seon-min, you're just like me.
-He cleans it out.

1409
00:53:05,982 --> 00:53:07,184
CLEARED

1410
00:53:07,250 --> 00:53:08,652
That dish is so clean.

1411
00:53:08,718 --> 00:53:09,920
That was amazing.

1412
00:53:09,986 --> 00:53:12,022
There's not even a trace.

1413
00:53:12,088 --> 00:53:12,923
Wait a second.

1414
00:53:12,989 --> 00:53:14,658
-Did he eat the dish?
-Is the dish there?

1415
00:53:14,724 --> 00:53:17,060
-I think he took a bite.
-I almost did.

1416
00:53:17,394 --> 00:53:19,729
MOVED

1417
00:53:20,063 --> 00:53:21,164
I won't be able to choose.

1418
00:53:21,231 --> 00:53:24,568
-It's really hard.
-Gosh, really? What will you do?

1419
00:53:24,634 --> 00:53:26,236
Try that, Nam-no.

1420
00:53:26,303 --> 00:53:28,004
-May I eat it?
-Yes, go ahead.

1421
00:53:28,071 --> 00:53:29,739
-While he thinks.
-Is there fried rice?

1422
00:53:29,806 --> 00:53:31,341
-Keep thinking.
-Yes, keep thinking.

1423
00:53:31,408 --> 00:53:34,578
-COKUN can't seem to decide.
-Wow.

1424
00:53:37,847 --> 00:53:39,482
He's eating his meal.

1425
00:53:39,549 --> 00:53:41,084
-Did he eat the shell?
-Gosh.

1426
00:53:41,518 --> 00:53:43,553
AMAZED…

1427
00:53:43,620 --> 00:53:47,157
Sam and Ho-young share some together.

1428
00:53:47,224 --> 00:53:49,559
They may be wearing chef's jackets,

1429
00:53:49,626 --> 00:53:51,294
but they look like two roommates.

1430
00:53:52,362 --> 00:53:55,932
They cook in a pan and eat out of it too.

1431
00:53:55,999 --> 00:53:58,101
They return home from work and go,

1432
00:53:58,168 --> 00:54:00,570
"Great work, my friend. Let's eat."

1433
00:54:00,637 --> 00:54:02,405
And they start eating from the pan.

1434
00:54:02,472 --> 00:54:04,507
Even the fact that they're silent.
It's so accurate.

1435
00:54:05,542 --> 00:54:08,111
Yes, you're starved and exhausted.

1436
00:54:08,178 --> 00:54:10,013
Tell us what it was like, chefs.

1437
00:54:10,580 --> 00:54:13,116
This is the best dish Poong has ever made.

1438
00:54:13,650 --> 00:54:15,285
AGREED

1439
00:54:15,352 --> 00:54:17,721
I even wanted to eat the shell.

1440
00:54:18,955 --> 00:54:20,790
And that touch of <i>doenjang</i>

1441
00:54:20,857 --> 00:54:23,260
<i>added a nice base flavor.</i>

1442
00:54:23,326 --> 00:54:24,894
THE SUBTLE DOENJANG FLAVOR IS THE KEY

1443
00:54:24,961 --> 00:54:26,229
I think he's a genius.

1444
00:54:26,296 --> 00:54:27,597
Nam-no?

1445
00:54:27,664 --> 00:54:28,999
I'm utterly shocked.

1446
00:54:29,065 --> 00:54:31,901
It'll be hard to find pu phat phong curry

1447
00:54:31,968 --> 00:54:33,670
that can beat this.

1448
00:54:33,737 --> 00:54:35,972
I had a bite
while passing it on to Nam-no.

1449
00:54:36,039 --> 00:54:39,209
Back in university, my roommate was Thai,

1450
00:54:39,276 --> 00:54:40,410
a guy named Phassakorn.

1451
00:54:40,477 --> 00:54:42,512
-In America?
-And he loved bell peppers.

1452
00:54:42,579 --> 00:54:46,316
Whenever I made him food,
he'd eat it and say thanks, right?

1453
00:54:46,383 --> 00:54:48,818
-He said, "Pepp <i>khun khrap</i>."
-Pepp <i>khun khrap</i>?

1454
00:54:50,220 --> 00:54:51,955
-That was the point.
-He told the story…

1455
00:54:52,022 --> 00:54:54,257
-…just to do the "Pepp <i>khun khrap</i>."
-That build-up.

1456
00:54:54,324 --> 00:54:56,826
What's this
about how his roommate was Thai?

1457
00:54:56,893 --> 00:54:59,129
That was a long story.

1458
00:54:59,195 --> 00:55:01,631
So was your roommate actually Thai?

1459
00:55:01,698 --> 00:55:03,500
-So that's true.
-And his name.

1460
00:55:04,134 --> 00:55:05,101
NICE ONE

1461
00:55:05,168 --> 00:55:07,470
Please try Chef Sung-jun's dish too.

1462
00:55:09,939 --> 00:55:10,974
Mmm.

1463
00:55:12,509 --> 00:55:14,344
I'M A DISHWASHER TOO

1464
00:55:15,278 --> 00:55:16,179
Whoa!

1465
00:55:17,247 --> 00:55:19,749
-What's up with you two today?
-They all ate, though.

1466
00:55:19,816 --> 00:55:23,253
I live in the southern United States now,

1467
00:55:23,320 --> 00:55:26,790
-so I know this flavor very well.
-Yes, of course.

1468
00:55:26,856 --> 00:55:27,724
And this is perfect.

1469
00:55:29,292 --> 00:55:30,293
Perfect?

1470
00:55:30,360 --> 00:55:32,862
Even Eddie said this was perfect.

1471
00:55:32,929 --> 00:55:34,964
I understood what you meant.

1472
00:55:35,031 --> 00:55:37,701
There's a difference between a dish
and a culinary creation.

1473
00:55:38,668 --> 00:55:44,307
The subtle yet smoky flavor
balances out the entire dish.

1474
00:55:44,374 --> 00:55:47,410
This is also the best dish
Sung-jun has ever made here.

1475
00:55:47,477 --> 00:55:50,447
-This is bad news.
-What's he going to do?

1476
00:55:50,513 --> 00:55:52,382
IT'S SO GOOD

1477
00:55:52,449 --> 00:55:54,584
-It's so addictive.
-I don't know what a crab boil is, but…

1478
00:55:54,651 --> 00:55:57,253
They're enjoying it too much.

1479
00:55:57,320 --> 00:55:58,655
You're still on the curry?

1480
00:55:58,722 --> 00:56:01,157
Eat that and go to sleep.
You have work tomorrow.

1481
00:56:01,224 --> 00:56:04,094
-Go to sleep. You have work.
-Let me have some too.

1482
00:56:04,160 --> 00:56:06,262
THE DISHWASHER FRIEND JOINS THE ROOM

1483
00:56:06,329 --> 00:56:08,064
They're both on fire.

1484
00:56:08,131 --> 00:56:09,966
-How's he going to choose?
-Right?

1485
00:56:10,033 --> 00:56:11,434
TORMENTED

1486
00:56:11,501 --> 00:56:14,037
-One of the two.
-You can choose only one of the two.

1487
00:56:14,104 --> 00:56:15,538
<i>Press one for Sung-jun</i>

1488
00:56:16,573 --> 00:56:17,674
<i>and two for Poong.</i>

1489
00:56:19,175 --> 00:56:20,643
Make your decision

1490
00:56:20,710 --> 00:56:22,712
and press the button!

1491
00:56:22,779 --> 00:56:24,547
This one had a stronger flavor.

1492
00:56:24,614 --> 00:56:26,583
-Did you press one? You did.
-I did.

1493
00:56:27,417 --> 00:56:29,152
-Can't wait to see the result.
-Me neither.

1494
00:56:29,219 --> 00:56:32,255
-Who won this round?
-All right.

1495
00:56:32,322 --> 00:56:34,157
Let's find out the result.

1496
00:56:34,224 --> 00:56:35,458
Show Me the Crab.

1497
00:56:35,525 --> 00:56:39,295
Show Me the Crab.
Who'll be thanked by COKUN?

1498
00:56:39,362 --> 00:56:41,131
If Poong wins, it'll be his 11th victory.

1499
00:56:41,197 --> 00:56:43,466
If Sung-jun wins, it'll be his tenth win.

1500
00:56:43,533 --> 00:56:45,301
Who did COKUN choose?

1501
00:56:45,368 --> 00:56:46,669
Who was it?

1502
00:56:46,736 --> 00:56:50,240
They cooked with blue crabs,
your favorite ingredient!

1503
00:56:50,306 --> 00:56:51,608
Show Me the Crab.

1504
00:56:51,674 --> 00:56:54,177
Will it be Poong or Sung-jun?

1505
00:56:54,244 --> 00:56:55,178
Who won?

1506
00:56:56,012 --> 00:56:57,680
-Who won?
-Who is it?

1507
00:56:59,983 --> 00:57:01,050
Who won?

1508
00:57:01,217 --> 00:57:03,386
KIM POONG WINS

1509
00:57:03,620 --> 00:57:05,555
KIM POONG'S
PU PHAT POONG CURRY, COKUN KHRAP WINS

1510
00:57:05,622 --> 00:57:06,790
Kim Poong wins!

1511
00:57:06,856 --> 00:57:08,658
-My gosh.
-It's Kim Poong.

1512
00:57:08,725 --> 00:57:10,827
-It was close.
-Sung-jun did well too.

1513
00:57:11,094 --> 00:57:12,929
IT WAS A NECK-AND-NECK ROUND

1514
00:57:12,996 --> 00:57:14,431
It was so hard to choose.

1515
00:57:14,497 --> 00:57:16,900
-It was so hard.
-I see.

1516
00:57:16,966 --> 00:57:19,969
It was as hard as making judgments
for <i>Show Me the Money.</i>

1517
00:57:20,036 --> 00:57:21,404
It was that hard?

1518
00:57:21,471 --> 00:57:24,741
If I could only eat one dish,
I'd probably eat this.

1519
00:57:26,276 --> 00:57:30,480
-Why didn't you choose that?
-But that wasn't my standard.

1520
00:57:30,547 --> 00:57:34,617
-Which dish satisfied my appetite more?
-That's true.

1521
00:57:34,684 --> 00:57:38,755
First, he changed
my bad experience with bell peppers.

1522
00:57:38,822 --> 00:57:40,990
And whenever I had this curry,

1523
00:57:41,057 --> 00:57:42,892
I've always thought it was a bit lacking.

1524
00:57:42,959 --> 00:57:46,129
But the crab was so tender and he managed
to really bring out that flavor.

1525
00:57:46,196 --> 00:57:47,664
That was why I chose him.

1526
00:57:47,730 --> 00:57:49,065
POONG'S DISH WAS MORE IDEAL

1527
00:57:49,132 --> 00:57:51,668
Can you give him a COKUN Khrap?

1528
00:57:51,734 --> 00:57:55,305
-COKUN Khrap.
-COKUN Khrap.

1529
00:57:55,572 --> 00:57:57,040
PEPP KHUN KHRAP

1530
00:57:57,106 --> 00:58:00,543
Please put a star on Poong!

1531
00:58:01,311 --> 00:58:03,313
CODE KUNST chose his dish.

1532
00:58:03,379 --> 00:58:05,882
This is his 11th win,

1533
00:58:05,949 --> 00:58:07,550
making him the joint second place.

1534
00:58:08,818 --> 00:58:09,752
That's fascinating.

1535
00:58:10,854 --> 00:58:12,322
He took Sung-jun down.

1536
00:58:12,989 --> 00:58:17,126
<i>The second round of cook-off</i>
<i>played with CODE KUNST's ingredients.</i>

1537
00:58:17,193 --> 00:58:19,662
Why can't we stop him from dancing?

1538
00:58:19,729 --> 00:58:20,697
Chef Jung Ho-young.

1539
00:58:20,763 --> 00:58:23,166
Why can't we stop him from making jokes?

1540
00:58:23,233 --> 00:58:25,869
Chef Son Jong-won.

1541
00:58:25,935 --> 00:58:28,872
Chef Ho-young has been on a roll lately

1542
00:58:28,938 --> 00:58:30,039
with four consecutive wins.

1543
00:58:30,106 --> 00:58:31,474
-Wow.
-Wow. Four straight wins?

1544
00:58:31,541 --> 00:58:32,976
-Really?
-Yes.

1545
00:58:33,042 --> 00:58:34,811
Until recently,

1546
00:58:34,878 --> 00:58:37,547
I wasn't on good terms with my wife
because I had lost so much,

1547
00:58:37,614 --> 00:58:39,148
-Right.
-…but lately,

1548
00:58:39,215 --> 00:58:41,251
things got better
after those consecutive wins.

1549
00:58:41,818 --> 00:58:45,321
-This was that important?
-We just received

1550
00:58:45,388 --> 00:58:47,390
something Chef Jong-won said.

1551
00:58:47,457 --> 00:58:48,491
-What did he say?
-What is it?

1552
00:58:48,558 --> 00:58:51,728
"Chef Ho-young seems to be
in his transition period lately."

1553
00:58:51,794 --> 00:58:52,729
-Why?
-What?

1554
00:58:52,795 --> 00:58:54,397
What do you mean by this?

1555
00:58:54,464 --> 00:58:56,499
It may sound impolite, but I think

1556
00:58:56,566 --> 00:58:59,235
-he must've forgotten where he started.
-Forgotten?

1557
00:59:00,336 --> 00:59:02,672
I still remember
his "One, two, three, Fry."

1558
00:59:03,239 --> 00:59:05,542
-He's been too focused on cooking,
-Yes.

1559
00:59:05,608 --> 00:59:07,544
…and his dance isn't as good.

1560
00:59:09,012 --> 00:59:10,013
Actually,

1561
00:59:10,079 --> 00:59:12,282
I've also been thinking a lot.

1562
00:59:12,348 --> 00:59:15,084
HE'S TALKING ABOUT DANCING, NOT COOKING

1563
00:59:15,151 --> 00:59:20,156
I also find it unfortunate that
I haven't done anything to beat that.

1564
00:59:20,223 --> 00:59:22,458
It's time I developed a new dance.

1565
00:59:22,525 --> 00:59:25,862
-"Develop"?
-He wants to develop his dance.

1566
00:59:25,929 --> 00:59:29,732
You should be developing recipes,
not dance moves.

1567
00:59:29,799 --> 00:59:32,368
But it's not just me.
Chef Jong-won also became ambitious

1568
00:59:32,435 --> 00:59:35,004
-about throwing jokes.
-He's too ambitious.

1569
00:59:35,071 --> 00:59:38,508
He keeps doing it, thinking it's funny
because Jung-hwan says so.

1570
00:59:38,575 --> 00:59:41,578
He's not that funny either.

1571
00:59:41,644 --> 00:59:42,478
That's true.

1572
00:59:42,745 --> 00:59:43,713
RIP, PEPP KHUN KHRAP…

1573
00:59:44,047 --> 00:59:45,648
…TRIGGERED

1574
00:59:45,715 --> 00:59:47,483
-How feisty.
-They're on each other's nerves.

1575
00:59:47,550 --> 00:59:50,420
They each went after
their biggest weaknesses.

1576
00:59:50,486 --> 00:59:52,088
-Hitting each other's sore spots.
-Wait.

1577
00:59:52,155 --> 00:59:55,458
Chef Ho-young has had a few awesome dances

1578
00:59:55,525 --> 00:59:57,026
but he lost every time he did.

1579
00:59:59,095 --> 01:00:00,630
<i>That's something to point out.</i>

1580
01:00:00,697 --> 01:00:04,867
He realized it's better
to dance less and win a star.

1581
01:00:04,934 --> 01:00:08,471
The worst day is the day
when I don't dance and still lose.

1582
01:00:08,538 --> 01:00:11,307
-That's such an awful day.
-That's the worst.

1583
01:00:11,374 --> 01:00:13,242
-Zero accomplishment.
-It's depressing.

1584
01:00:13,309 --> 01:00:16,145
Anyway, you two
are about to make fruit dishes.

1585
01:00:16,212 --> 01:00:19,716
Jong-won, I hear
you're very confident about this.

1586
01:00:19,782 --> 01:00:21,918
I think this order was made for me.

1587
01:00:21,985 --> 01:00:25,021
I'm the type who enjoys everything warm.

1588
01:00:25,088 --> 01:00:27,824
I'd cut up a watermelon
and leave it in my car.

1589
01:00:27,890 --> 01:00:30,360
By the time the shoot ends,
it's pretty lukewarm.

1590
01:00:31,894 --> 01:00:34,597
-And I eat that.
-You eat warm watermelon?

1591
01:00:34,664 --> 01:00:37,200
Do you leave it on your passenger seat
and turn on the seat warmer?

1592
01:00:37,533 --> 01:00:39,469
WARMING WATERMELON WITH THE SEAT WARMER(?)

1593
01:00:39,535 --> 01:00:41,938
Do you like warm fruit too, Ho-young?

1594
01:00:42,005 --> 01:00:45,975
I unplugged my fridge
to eat my fruits warm.

1595
01:00:46,042 --> 01:00:47,276
To eat them warm?

1596
01:00:47,343 --> 01:00:49,846
-Stop with this nonsense.
-Things are tense.

1597
01:00:49,912 --> 01:00:52,615
THE MIND GAME GETS TAINTED WITH BLUFFS

1598
01:00:52,682 --> 01:00:54,517
Just tell us about your dishes.

1599
01:00:55,785 --> 01:00:57,854
CHEF JONG-WON'S INGREDIENTS:
PLUM, PEACH, APRICOT JAM, QUINCE CHEONG

1600
01:00:57,920 --> 01:00:59,956
BREAD, CHICKEN BREAST HAM, ROMAINE,
COFFEE BEAN, PROTEIN POWDER, AND MORE

1601
01:01:00,023 --> 01:01:01,958
What are you making, Chef Jong-won?

1602
01:01:02,025 --> 01:01:04,327
The name of my dish is this.

1603
01:01:04,394 --> 01:01:07,463
Can You Stop My Plums?

1604
01:01:09,666 --> 01:01:11,067
His signature line.

1605
01:01:11,134 --> 01:01:12,935
Can you stop me?

1606
01:01:13,002 --> 01:01:14,504
He's saying it to Ho-young.

1607
01:01:14,570 --> 01:01:15,538
That's deep.

1608
01:01:15,605 --> 01:01:18,474
I'm going to grill some plums.

1609
01:01:18,541 --> 01:01:19,609
Plums?

1610
01:01:19,676 --> 01:01:21,377
Have you had warm plums?

1611
01:01:21,444 --> 01:01:23,646
-No, not plums.
-Not plums?

1612
01:01:23,713 --> 01:01:26,816
I'll grill it and juice it to make sauce.

1613
01:01:26,883 --> 01:01:27,717
Grill and then juice?

1614
01:01:27,784 --> 01:01:29,018
I'll also make plum compote

1615
01:01:29,085 --> 01:01:31,020
and a salad with grilled peaches.

1616
01:01:31,087 --> 01:01:34,724
Along with those,
I'll serve <i>cervelas en </i>brioche,

1617
01:01:34,791 --> 01:01:37,493
a classic French dish rooted in Lyon.

1618
01:01:37,560 --> 01:01:41,431
It's brioche stuffed
with circular <i>cervelas</i> sausage,

1619
01:01:42,031 --> 01:01:44,701
so I'll try and make that

1620
01:01:44,767 --> 01:01:47,336
-with chicken breast ham.
-Ham.

1621
01:01:47,403 --> 01:01:49,038
That doesn't sound bad.

1622
01:01:49,105 --> 01:01:51,908
If I have enough time, I'll serve it
with a beverage made with coffee.

1623
01:01:53,409 --> 01:01:55,845
I'm already leaning toward him.

1624
01:01:56,779 --> 01:01:58,748
CHEF HO-YOUNG'S INGREDIENTS:
BANANA, PEACH, PLUM

1625
01:01:58,815 --> 01:02:00,850
PINEAPPLE, BREAD,
MILK, CREAM CHEESE, SEASONED SALT

1626
01:02:00,917 --> 01:02:03,419
And Chef Ho-young, what will you make?

1627
01:02:04,287 --> 01:02:06,556
I'm going to make a warm fruit dish.

1628
01:02:06,622 --> 01:02:11,394
You said you didn't have a good memory
of eating warm banana,

1629
01:02:11,461 --> 01:02:13,329
so I want to face that

1630
01:02:13,396 --> 01:02:15,998
-and make a warm banana dish.
-You're facing that?

1631
01:02:16,065 --> 01:02:18,000
-Memory cover-up.
-Next,

1632
01:02:18,067 --> 01:02:20,369
I'll grill peaches in butter and sugar,

1633
01:02:20,436 --> 01:02:22,772
then serve it
with custard sauce and cream cheese mix.

1634
01:02:22,839 --> 01:02:25,341
-I'll prepare two different dishes.
-I see.

1635
01:02:25,408 --> 01:02:28,144
-One banana dish, one peach dish.
-Peach dish.

1636
01:02:28,211 --> 01:02:31,280
Who's going to be chosen?

1637
01:02:31,347 --> 01:02:36,085
Let us begin the round,
"Why Can't We Stop Warm Fruit?"

1638
01:02:36,152 --> 01:02:38,454
Why can't anyone stop Jong-won?

1639
01:02:38,521 --> 01:02:41,791
The current first place of the show,
Chef Son Jong-won.

1640
01:02:41,858 --> 01:02:45,194
He probably can't stop Jong-won,
but he can still eat.

1641
01:02:45,261 --> 01:02:47,964
Chef Jung Ho-young
with four consecutive wins.

1642
01:02:48,531 --> 01:02:50,700
Which one will capture COKUN's heart?

1643
01:02:50,767 --> 01:02:54,237
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1644
01:02:55,271 --> 01:02:57,073
THINK OUTSIDE THE BOX
FOR THIS FRUIT ROUND!

1645
01:02:57,640 --> 01:02:58,941
They're making fruit dishes.

1646
01:02:59,008 --> 01:03:00,810
Custard.

1647
01:03:00,877 --> 01:03:03,746
He's using butter first. Butter and sugar.

1648
01:03:04,380 --> 01:03:05,214
What's he doing?

1649
01:03:05,281 --> 01:03:07,650
JONG-WON STARTS WITH SUGAR & WATER

1650
01:03:07,717 --> 01:03:09,619
Yes, syrup.

1651
01:03:10,386 --> 01:03:11,287
-Plum.
-Plum.

1652
01:03:11,354 --> 01:03:14,123
Plums, bananas, peaches.

1653
01:03:14,190 --> 01:03:15,925
It feels like a dessert round.

1654
01:03:15,992 --> 01:03:18,060
They're using plums and peaches.

1655
01:03:18,127 --> 01:03:21,831
COKUN, you said
you didn't prefer grilled bananas.

1656
01:03:21,898 --> 01:03:25,668
I had it once
when I went to Southeast Asia,

1657
01:03:25,735 --> 01:03:27,503
and it was too rich for me.

1658
01:03:27,570 --> 01:03:29,939
It gets creamy,
so it must've felt too rich.

1659
01:03:30,006 --> 01:03:32,675
Ho-young better do
a great job with bananas.

1660
01:03:32,742 --> 01:03:34,477
He's cooking with a risk.

1661
01:03:34,544 --> 01:03:35,611
But on the other hand,

1662
01:03:35,678 --> 01:03:38,114
bananas are my favorite fruit.

1663
01:03:38,181 --> 01:03:40,483
-I see.
-But when I eat it as is.

1664
01:03:40,550 --> 01:03:41,551
Just not grilled.

1665
01:03:41,617 --> 01:03:43,986
But what if he can grill it warmly?

1666
01:03:44,053 --> 01:03:45,888
He's making a dish using bread.

1667
01:03:45,955 --> 01:03:48,624
I'm using it to deep-fry pineapple.

1668
01:03:49,292 --> 01:03:51,027
-You're using pineapple too?
-That sounds good.

1669
01:03:51,093 --> 01:03:52,428
-Whoa.
-He'll use many different fruits.

1670
01:03:52,795 --> 01:03:54,363
GRIND BREAD INTO BREADCRUMBS

1671
01:03:54,430 --> 01:03:55,765
Shake it. Is that okay?

1672
01:03:55,832 --> 01:03:57,600
It'd be nice if someone shook it.

1673
01:03:57,667 --> 01:03:58,835
-Someone.
-Someone.

1674
01:03:58,901 --> 01:03:59,902
Get moving, Someone.

1675
01:04:00,536 --> 01:04:01,637
BUT THEIR CHEMISTRY IS LONG GONE

1676
01:04:01,704 --> 01:04:03,673
I think it's automatic.

1677
01:04:04,207 --> 01:04:05,208
SORRY

1678
01:04:05,274 --> 01:04:06,676
Can someone shake it?

1679
01:04:06,742 --> 01:04:07,743
DANG IT!

1680
01:04:07,810 --> 01:04:08,744
Someone shake it.

1681
01:04:08,811 --> 01:04:11,480
LOVE HIM OR HATE HIM,
STILL CAN'T LEAVE HIM

1682
01:04:11,547 --> 01:04:13,316
THE MOVED SINGLE & ACCUSTOMED MARRIED GUY

1683
01:04:13,649 --> 01:04:15,418
BUT IS IT OKAY
TO LEAVE THE BLENDER WITH HIM?

1684
01:04:15,484 --> 01:04:17,086
That's good.

1685
01:04:17,787 --> 01:04:18,888
Thanks.

1686
01:04:19,121 --> 01:04:20,656
A BANANA HAS SPLIT

1687
01:04:21,090 --> 01:04:23,526
JONG-WON IS STILL CHOPPING PLUMS

1688
01:04:24,260 --> 01:04:25,995
He's using a lot of plums.

1689
01:04:26,329 --> 01:04:28,030
CIRCULAR, RECTANGULAR

1690
01:04:28,297 --> 01:04:29,832
He's grilling the plums.

1691
01:04:29,899 --> 01:04:33,336
-Plums.
-He halves them and grills them like meat.

1692
01:04:33,402 --> 01:04:34,570
Or like veggies.

1693
01:04:34,637 --> 01:04:37,006
What will plums taste like when grilled?

1694
01:04:37,974 --> 01:04:40,910
I've had fried and grilled bananas,

1695
01:04:40,977 --> 01:04:42,411
and of course, pineapples too.

1696
01:04:42,478 --> 01:04:45,281
I'll use this quince <i>cheong</i>
made by Ho-jin, the fourth grader.

1697
01:04:45,348 --> 01:04:47,583
-I see.
-He's using that?

1698
01:04:47,650 --> 01:04:48,985
I finally get to use it.

1699
01:04:49,051 --> 01:04:51,053
Finally. He'd be thrilled.

1700
01:04:51,120 --> 01:04:52,788
-Thanks, Ho-jin.
-He'll be so excited.

1701
01:04:52,855 --> 01:04:54,557
-Thanks, Ho-jin.
-Your mom's friend's son.

1702
01:04:54,624 --> 01:04:56,259
-He's in middle school now.
-Middle school?

1703
01:04:56,325 --> 01:04:58,261
-It's been three years.
-Are you watching this?

1704
01:04:58,461 --> 01:04:59,762
SIMPLE SYRUP

1705
01:04:59,829 --> 01:05:00,963
Syrup, done.

1706
01:05:01,631 --> 01:05:04,400
-He's done with the syrup already?
-Yes, he's cooling it down.

1707
01:05:05,067 --> 01:05:06,202
You're grinding a banana?

1708
01:05:06,269 --> 01:05:08,571
-Banana.
-You're blending a banana.

1709
01:05:08,638 --> 01:05:10,339
Egg. Egg yolk.

1710
01:05:10,773 --> 01:05:13,276
STARTS MAKING BANANA CUSTARD

1711
01:05:13,342 --> 01:05:15,144
Why don't you add a hint of pineapple?

1712
01:05:15,211 --> 01:05:18,047
A bit of it can add some sourness.

1713
01:05:18,114 --> 01:05:18,948
SUGAR

1714
01:05:19,015 --> 01:05:19,916
MILK

1715
01:05:19,982 --> 01:05:21,317
STARCH

1716
01:05:22,051 --> 01:05:23,252
You don't have lemon.

1717
01:05:23,319 --> 01:05:24,387
-Should I?
-Yes.

1718
01:05:24,453 --> 01:05:25,922
I think he'll listen to Poong.

1719
01:05:25,988 --> 01:05:27,657
Let me use just the pineapple juice.

1720
01:05:27,924 --> 01:05:31,627
JONG-WON SNATCHES THE PINEAPPLE FIRST!

1721
01:05:31,694 --> 01:05:33,596
-Just the juice.
-Excuse me.

1722
01:05:33,663 --> 01:05:35,698
Chop a tiny piece and throw it in.

1723
01:05:35,765 --> 01:05:39,402
He wasn't planning to use it,
but he decided to listen to Poong?

1724
01:05:39,468 --> 01:05:41,070
-What's going on?
-Adding lemon can

1725
01:05:41,137 --> 01:05:44,173
balance out the egg smell,
but he didn't have it.

1726
01:05:44,240 --> 01:05:46,442
-So use pineapple instead?
-As an alternative.

1727
01:05:46,509 --> 01:05:49,378
-I see.
-My faith in him is high after the curry.

1728
01:05:49,445 --> 01:05:51,213
It's like that now, right?

1729
01:05:51,280 --> 01:05:53,950
They would've been reluctant in the past.

1730
01:05:54,016 --> 01:05:56,285
-But Poong shares his opinion confidently.
-Yes.

1731
01:05:57,219 --> 01:06:00,690
2. STIR AND DOUBLE-BOIL BANANA, EGG YOLK,
SUGAR, MILK, STARCH, AND PINEAPPLE

1732
01:06:00,923 --> 01:06:01,757
SO HOT

1733
01:06:02,725 --> 01:06:05,094
Plums get stuck when you grill them too?

1734
01:06:05,161 --> 01:06:05,995
It's soft.

1735
01:06:06,062 --> 01:06:07,763
He's juicing them next.

1736
01:06:07,830 --> 01:06:10,099
-You're juicing them after grilling them?
-What's that?

1737
01:06:10,166 --> 01:06:12,168
-A juicer.
-A juicer.

1738
01:06:12,234 --> 01:06:14,770
Is it different
if you juice them after grilling?

1739
01:06:14,837 --> 01:06:16,605
It becomes much more flavorful.

1740
01:06:18,074 --> 01:06:19,842
-Wow.
-So he's not using it as is?

1741
01:06:20,543 --> 01:06:22,345
It'll have that smoky flavor,

1742
01:06:22,411 --> 01:06:24,947
-and have a stronger smell and sweetness.
-I can't even imagine.

1743
01:06:25,014 --> 01:06:27,850
-They're grilled?
-They look so sweet.

1744
01:06:28,517 --> 01:06:31,320
-He's using peaches too.
-He said he'd use them.

1745
01:06:31,387 --> 01:06:34,724
They're using the same fruits,
but Jong-won isn't using bananas.

1746
01:06:34,790 --> 01:06:36,525
-He's not.
-I see.

1747
01:06:37,026 --> 01:06:39,562
-Gosh, even peaches?
-He grills peaches too.

1748
01:06:39,628 --> 01:06:40,763
This is very new.

1749
01:06:40,830 --> 01:06:42,498
Next, bread. Is that brioche?

1750
01:06:42,565 --> 01:06:45,167
-No, it's just bread.
-It was in the freezer.

1751
01:06:45,234 --> 01:06:47,336
It finally came out after a long time.

1752
01:06:47,403 --> 01:06:49,839
-It finally saw daylight again.
-And ham.

1753
01:06:49,905 --> 01:06:51,807
-He's using the canned ham.
-It's chicken ham.

1754
01:06:51,874 --> 01:06:55,911
He made a hole in the bread
and put the ham inside.

1755
01:06:55,978 --> 01:06:58,014
-The chicken breast ham.
-Chicken ham.

1756
01:06:58,080 --> 01:06:59,181
Chicken ham.

1757
01:06:59,248 --> 01:07:01,917
This canned ham
is quite similar to <i>cervelas.</i>

1758
01:07:02,852 --> 01:07:03,819
AMAZED

1759
01:07:03,886 --> 01:07:06,655
It's such a classical dish,

1760
01:07:06,722 --> 01:07:08,491
but it's ridiculous
that he's making it here.

1761
01:07:09,625 --> 01:07:11,527
HO-YOUNG MAKES FRYING BATTER

1762
01:07:11,927 --> 01:07:14,096
AND HE NEEDS BREADCRUMBS…

1763
01:07:14,563 --> 01:07:16,399
THE BLENDER WON'T POP OUT?

1764
01:07:16,465 --> 01:07:19,368
-The blender, Ho-young.
-What is it?

1765
01:07:19,435 --> 01:07:21,437
-It won't come out.
-Why not?

1766
01:07:21,504 --> 01:07:23,205
He flips the whole machine!

1767
01:07:24,106 --> 01:07:25,007
WHO DID THIS?

1768
01:07:25,074 --> 01:07:26,942
He just pours it into the bowl.

1769
01:07:27,009 --> 01:07:28,744
It's supposed to come off.

1770
01:07:28,811 --> 01:07:30,746
-Why won't it come off?
-Why not?

1771
01:07:30,813 --> 01:07:32,782
Chef Sam was the last one who touched it.

1772
01:07:32,848 --> 01:07:34,417
Why me?

1773
01:07:34,483 --> 01:07:35,351
He touched it last.

1774
01:07:35,418 --> 01:07:37,386
-He used it.
-You're right!

1775
01:07:37,453 --> 01:07:38,387
It was him.

1776
01:07:38,454 --> 01:07:40,689
He doesn't care about the next person.

1777
01:07:41,891 --> 01:07:44,293
HE SHOULDN'T HAVE
LET SAM TOUCH THE BLENDER

1778
01:07:44,360 --> 01:07:47,663
The Canine incidents happen for a reason.

1779
01:07:47,730 --> 01:07:49,532
Are you coating the pineapple
with breadcrumbs to fry them?

1780
01:07:49,598 --> 01:07:51,734
-That's right.
-That sounds good.

1781
01:07:51,801 --> 01:07:53,269
It reminds me

1782
01:07:53,335 --> 01:07:54,804
of Poong's deep-fried melon.

1783
01:07:54,870 --> 01:07:57,373
-You're right.
-You did that, right? It's that.

1784
01:07:57,440 --> 01:08:01,177
-He's the first to deep-fry a melon.
-He's coating it with breadcrumbs.

1785
01:08:01,243 --> 01:08:05,881
-I ended up inspiring many.
-You did.

1786
01:08:06,248 --> 01:08:08,684
THE ROAD TO KIM POONG,
ALL ROADS LEAD TO HIM

1787
01:08:08,751 --> 01:08:12,521
-It seemed familiar.
-We bounce ideas off each other.

1788
01:08:12,855 --> 01:08:14,390
SIMPLE SYRUP

1789
01:08:16,192 --> 01:08:18,494
Coffee.

1790
01:08:18,561 --> 01:08:20,196
SETTING UP THE DRIPPER IN AN ICED BOWL?

1791
01:08:20,262 --> 01:08:23,599
I know we're all busy, but we should
all relax for a cup of coffee.

1792
01:08:23,666 --> 01:08:25,034
GOOD VIBES

1793
01:08:25,100 --> 01:08:26,569
-He's relaxed.
-You seem relaxed.

1794
01:08:26,635 --> 01:08:27,870
That's like a coffee ad.

1795
01:08:27,937 --> 01:08:30,406
He's trying to land an ad gig.

1796
01:08:30,473 --> 01:08:33,175
He now knows how the industry works.

1797
01:08:33,242 --> 01:08:34,743
He really does.

1798
01:08:34,810 --> 01:08:35,878
He's a scary guy.

1799
01:08:35,945 --> 01:08:40,049
He knows all he needs
is a single cut of him looking relaxed.

1800
01:08:40,116 --> 01:08:42,485
He gives that good vibe.

1801
01:08:42,551 --> 01:08:45,054
Anyone can tell
that he's going after an ad gig.

1802
01:08:45,554 --> 01:08:47,823
-A coffee ad.
-Even a coffee ad?

1803
01:08:47,890 --> 01:08:49,758
Ho-young can go for a peach ad.

1804
01:08:49,825 --> 01:08:51,093
What are you stirring so rapidly?

1805
01:08:51,160 --> 01:08:52,394
This? Custard.

1806
01:08:52,461 --> 01:08:54,330
It's done. You got it.

1807
01:08:54,396 --> 01:08:55,698
-It's thickening.
-It's creamy.

1808
01:08:55,764 --> 01:08:57,533
-I think it went well.
-It turned out nicely.

1809
01:08:57,600 --> 01:08:58,767
-Is it okay?
-It is.

1810
01:08:58,834 --> 01:09:00,669
-Is it sour too?
-Yes.

1811
01:09:00,836 --> 01:09:02,071
CREAM CHEESE

1812
01:09:02,671 --> 01:09:04,206
SOMEONE HELPS HIM QUIETLY

1813
01:09:04,273 --> 01:09:06,742
Wait, I think your peach is burning.

1814
01:09:06,809 --> 01:09:08,344
-It's burning?
-I think so.

1815
01:09:09,478 --> 01:09:11,680
SCREAM!

1816
01:09:11,914 --> 01:09:14,950
HE PROBABLY DIDN'T WANT
ANYTHING THAT WARM

1817
01:09:15,017 --> 01:09:17,086
-Isn't that burnt?
-It's not.

1818
01:09:17,153 --> 01:09:19,355
No, that's brûlée.

1819
01:09:19,421 --> 01:09:21,557
They look like horseshoes.

1820
01:09:23,592 --> 01:09:24,727
Horseshoes?

1821
01:09:24,793 --> 01:09:26,562
Horse number one!

1822
01:09:26,629 --> 01:09:28,364
It reminds me of the brand Jordache.

1823
01:09:28,430 --> 01:09:29,832
Jordache?

1824
01:09:29,899 --> 01:09:31,333
That's ancient.

1825
01:09:31,400 --> 01:09:32,568
WHAT? IS? THAT?

1826
01:09:32,635 --> 01:09:34,170
Do only I know that brand?

1827
01:09:34,236 --> 01:09:35,905
-What is that?
-What's Jordache?

1828
01:09:35,971 --> 01:09:37,206
-A denim brand.
-You don't know?

1829
01:09:37,273 --> 01:09:38,474
-I don't.
-No.

1830
01:09:38,541 --> 01:09:39,441
-No.
-No.

1831
01:09:39,508 --> 01:09:40,442
I only know Levi's.

1832
01:09:40,509 --> 01:09:41,443
I know Matdasi, the condiment.

1833
01:09:42,144 --> 01:09:43,379
You don't know Jordache?

1834
01:09:43,712 --> 01:09:46,182
JONG-WON, BORN IN 1984, CUTS ANOTHER PLUM

1835
01:09:46,649 --> 01:09:49,585
THIS TIME, INTO MINI DICES!

1836
01:09:50,786 --> 01:09:51,987
He's deep-frying it.

1837
01:09:52,054 --> 01:09:53,856
Deep-fried pineapple.

1838
01:09:53,923 --> 01:09:55,224
He's frying the pineapple.

1839
01:09:56,025 --> 01:09:58,694
-It looks too hot.
-Isn't it too hot?

1840
01:09:58,761 --> 01:10:01,530
-They think it's too hot.
-It just needs to fry briefly.

1841
01:10:01,597 --> 01:10:03,532
-It'll burn.
-It won't.

1842
01:10:03,599 --> 01:10:05,568
-Oh, no.
-What if the pineapple melts?

1843
01:10:05,634 --> 01:10:06,535
It won't burn.

1844
01:10:06,602 --> 01:10:09,371
-It's perfect.
-It is perfect.

1845
01:10:09,438 --> 01:10:10,573
It's ready.

1846
01:10:10,639 --> 01:10:11,907
-Gosh.
-Just a little more.

1847
01:10:11,974 --> 01:10:15,411
-It's fine.
-They look like fried chicken.

1848
01:10:15,477 --> 01:10:18,647
-It looks hot.
-It looks delicious.

1849
01:10:18,714 --> 01:10:19,615
What's next?

1850
01:10:19,782 --> 01:10:20,916
THE SPLIT BANANA

1851
01:10:20,983 --> 01:10:23,752
He's grilling the banana with a torch.

1852
01:10:23,819 --> 01:10:25,354
Grilled banana.

1853
01:10:25,421 --> 01:10:28,657
-With sugar on top. It has to be good.
-It should be good.

1854
01:10:28,724 --> 01:10:29,892
What's next?

1855
01:10:29,959 --> 01:10:32,127
-Plum.
-He's using a plum too.

1856
01:10:32,194 --> 01:10:34,263
-He's grilling the plum too.
-Yes.

1857
01:10:34,330 --> 01:10:36,899
-Wait, those…
-The horseshoes.

1858
01:10:36,966 --> 01:10:39,168
It has turned dark.

1859
01:10:39,235 --> 01:10:42,938
He created two pairs of horseshoes,
one for a racehorse, one for a pony.

1860
01:10:43,005 --> 01:10:44,473
Horseshoes.

1861
01:10:44,540 --> 01:10:46,308
Please go check, Jung-hwan!

1862
01:10:46,375 --> 01:10:48,444
-Gosh.
-He has to try that.

1863
01:10:48,510 --> 01:10:49,545
He just sighed.

1864
01:10:49,612 --> 01:10:52,147
-I have no idea what that'd be like.
-Me neither.

1865
01:10:52,214 --> 01:10:54,283
I can't even guess what that'd be like.

1866
01:10:54,350 --> 01:10:56,852
It's hard to imagine the flavor.

1867
01:10:56,919 --> 01:10:59,188
JONG-WON IS DONE GRILLING PEACH TOO

1868
01:10:59,888 --> 01:11:02,558
Chef Jong-won slices the grilled peach.

1869
01:11:03,659 --> 01:11:05,861
And he cuts it with a mold.

1870
01:11:06,629 --> 01:11:09,632
This one looks great.
He grilled the peach.

1871
01:11:10,933 --> 01:11:12,401
WHAT ABOUT WHAT'S IN THE OVEN?

1872
01:11:12,835 --> 01:11:13,669
BREAD WITH CANNED HAM

1873
01:11:13,736 --> 01:11:15,337
Jong-won looks busy.

1874
01:11:15,404 --> 01:11:16,739
He's brushing butter on it.

1875
01:11:16,805 --> 01:11:18,107
He's putting in a lot of work.

1876
01:11:18,173 --> 01:11:19,041
And coffee's brewing.

1877
01:11:19,108 --> 01:11:21,343
It's going to take forever to finish.

1878
01:11:21,410 --> 01:11:24,413
He's putting in a lot of work
into that <i>cervelas en </i>brioche.

1879
01:11:25,581 --> 01:11:27,149
He toasts them again
after spreading butter on them.

1880
01:11:27,216 --> 01:11:29,785
Jong-won uses vegetables.

1881
01:11:29,852 --> 01:11:33,255
He just chopped romaine lettuce

1882
01:11:33,322 --> 01:11:36,225
because this dish
is often eaten with veggies like chicory.

1883
01:11:36,292 --> 01:11:38,594
-That salad. But he's using lettuce.
-I see.

1884
01:11:38,661 --> 01:11:40,729
-Is that the recipe?
-Yes, it's often served with salad.

1885
01:11:40,796 --> 01:11:42,298
He sticks to the tradition.

1886
01:11:42,364 --> 01:11:44,133
7. ADD SUGAR TO PLUM COMPOTE
AND BLEND WELL

1887
01:11:45,534 --> 01:11:49,271
Look at his techniques.

1888
01:11:49,338 --> 01:11:50,639
It's insane.

1889
01:11:50,706 --> 01:11:52,908
-I'm just mixing it.
-You are?

1890
01:11:52,975 --> 01:11:56,512
-He's now plating the salad.
-Under three minutes.

1891
01:11:56,578 --> 01:11:58,847
He tops the peach slices with lettuce.

1892
01:11:58,914 --> 01:12:00,716
-There's the plum sauce.
-The sauce.

1893
01:12:00,783 --> 01:12:03,719
The sauce. You grilled and juiced them.

1894
01:12:03,786 --> 01:12:06,789
-Yes, that was probably important.
-It's nice.

1895
01:12:07,823 --> 01:12:10,993
Do you think he can be stopped?

1896
01:12:11,060 --> 01:12:12,728
I don't think he can be stopped.

1897
01:12:12,795 --> 01:12:13,896
HE CAN'T BE STOPPED!

1898
01:12:13,962 --> 01:12:15,097
It must be good.

1899
01:12:15,164 --> 01:12:17,966
-Does it taste grilled?
-It's like eating

1900
01:12:18,033 --> 01:12:20,903
the crispy edges
of candied sweet potatoes.

1901
01:12:20,969 --> 01:12:22,438
-I see.
-And I can taste plums.

1902
01:12:22,504 --> 01:12:24,440
-Caramelized.
-It's been caramelized.

1903
01:12:25,207 --> 01:12:27,242
That's nice. It smells like jam.

1904
01:12:27,309 --> 01:12:29,144
He didn't even add sugar.

1905
01:12:29,878 --> 01:12:31,480
WHAT'S THE CHERRY ON TOP?

1906
01:12:31,914 --> 01:12:33,582
A SPOON OF PLUM COMPOTE

1907
01:12:34,683 --> 01:12:35,517
GLANCES

1908
01:12:35,818 --> 01:12:38,053
THE PEACH BRÛLÉE LOOKS FULLY COOKED

1909
01:12:38,320 --> 01:12:39,521
PLUM BRÛLÉE

1910
01:12:39,888 --> 01:12:43,892
6. PLACE PLUM BRÛLÉE AND PEACH BRÛLÉE
ON TOP OF BANANA CUSTARD

1911
01:12:43,959 --> 01:12:47,763
I think Ho-young's banana is done.

1912
01:12:47,830 --> 01:12:49,865
And he needs to plate the horseshoes.

1913
01:12:49,932 --> 01:12:51,600
And the fried pineapples.

1914
01:12:51,667 --> 01:12:52,568
-Jung-hwan.
-Yes?

1915
01:12:52,634 --> 01:12:54,903
Can you try
that leftover piece of pineapple?

1916
01:12:54,970 --> 01:12:55,804
Pine-fry?

1917
01:12:55,871 --> 01:12:57,106
-Yes.
-Pine-fry?

1918
01:12:57,172 --> 01:12:59,541
Pineapple fry.

1919
01:12:59,608 --> 01:13:01,677
-Should I dip it in here?
-It doesn't seem too hot.

1920
01:13:01,744 --> 01:13:03,112
So that's the dip.

1921
01:13:03,178 --> 01:13:06,248
This is the sauce
for the fried pineapples.

1922
01:13:06,315 --> 01:13:07,282
What's it like?

1923
01:13:07,349 --> 01:13:09,585
It's really hot!

1924
01:13:09,651 --> 01:13:11,487
-It is?
-Gosh.

1925
01:13:11,553 --> 01:13:13,922
Can you chew the pineapple?
Or did it turn into juice?

1926
01:13:13,989 --> 01:13:16,592
-The juice is so hot!
-Is it?

1927
01:13:16,658 --> 01:13:17,493
SEASONED SALT

1928
01:13:18,727 --> 01:13:20,129
It didn't melt away.

1929
01:13:20,195 --> 01:13:23,966
-The pineapple juice is so hot.
-It felt hot?

1930
01:13:24,032 --> 01:13:25,768
-It's nice.
-Is it?

1931
01:13:25,834 --> 01:13:27,703
I think he has a strong chance.

1932
01:13:27,770 --> 01:13:30,305
-Was it that good?
-Shouldn't it be cold?

1933
01:13:30,372 --> 01:13:32,441
-Is it still hot?
-It is.

1934
01:13:32,508 --> 01:13:34,443
Are you making cocktail?

1935
01:13:34,510 --> 01:13:37,112
He's been making lots of drinks recently.

1936
01:13:37,179 --> 01:13:39,415
-What's that? Coffee?
-What's that?

1937
01:13:39,481 --> 01:13:41,383
He's making cocktail with coffee.

1938
01:13:41,450 --> 01:13:42,551
HE'S MAKING COFFEE COCKTAIL

1939
01:13:42,885 --> 01:13:44,920
That was why he made it like that.

1940
01:13:46,321 --> 01:13:47,489
-Gosh.
-He's vying for an ad.

1941
01:13:47,556 --> 01:13:48,490
SWOONING

1942
01:13:48,690 --> 01:13:50,692
HOW GOOD DOES IT SMELL?

1943
01:13:50,759 --> 01:13:53,095
-The coffee smelled nice.
-Was it nice?

1944
01:13:53,162 --> 01:13:54,630
Is that an espresso martini?

1945
01:13:54,696 --> 01:13:55,964
Something like that.

1946
01:13:56,031 --> 01:13:59,067
-Espresso martini?
-Filter coffee martini.

1947
01:13:59,134 --> 01:14:00,702
-I see.
-Coffee martini.

1948
01:14:00,769 --> 01:14:02,671
One minute left.

1949
01:14:02,738 --> 01:14:04,773
-He's already done?
-Ho-young is done.

1950
01:14:05,274 --> 01:14:07,276
IT'S ADVANTAGEOUS TO FINISH FIRST
WHEN IT'S A WARM DISH!

1951
01:14:07,342 --> 01:14:09,311
He's adding syrup there?

1952
01:14:09,378 --> 01:14:12,448
That was what the syrup was for?

1953
01:14:12,514 --> 01:14:14,450
-Beer.
-Jong-won.

1954
01:14:14,516 --> 01:14:16,418
He needs to make foam
to make a proper coffee martini.

1955
01:14:16,485 --> 01:14:18,086
-Huh?
-He adds beer too.

1956
01:14:18,153 --> 01:14:19,988
-Be quiet.
-No foam means it's ruined.

1957
01:14:20,289 --> 01:14:21,156
SUNG-JUN'S THE WORST…

1958
01:14:21,223 --> 01:14:22,424
Do you have to see foam?

1959
01:14:22,491 --> 01:14:25,627
-White foam to float on top.
-And only then is it proper?

1960
01:14:25,694 --> 01:14:28,197
-He looks so cool!
-Will it make foam or not?

1961
01:14:28,964 --> 01:14:30,165
No foam, and it's over.

1962
01:14:30,232 --> 01:14:32,434
Chef Jong-won has been
good with his hands lately.

1963
01:14:32,501 --> 01:14:33,702
He's good.

1964
01:14:33,769 --> 01:14:34,903
He's a Son, like <i>son,</i> hand.

1965
01:14:34,970 --> 01:14:37,506
-It won't come off.
-It won't?

1966
01:14:37,573 --> 01:14:39,508
It came off.

1967
01:14:39,575 --> 01:14:41,510
-Okay.
-What will you do with it?

1968
01:14:41,577 --> 01:14:43,846
-It's a beverage.
-He's sifting it through a strainer.

1969
01:14:43,912 --> 01:14:44,947
-Put it through a strainer.
-It's coming out.

1970
01:14:45,013 --> 01:14:46,882
WILL IT MAKE FOAM?

1971
01:14:46,949 --> 01:14:47,983
-Whoa.
-For COKUN.

1972
01:14:48,050 --> 01:14:49,418
-Wow.
-It's working.

1973
01:14:50,953 --> 01:14:52,888
-Fifteen seconds left.
-Fifteen!

1974
01:14:52,955 --> 01:14:54,690
-Please wrap up.
-There's foam.

1975
01:14:54,756 --> 01:14:56,124
-This is insane.
-No way.

1976
01:14:56,425 --> 01:14:59,228
GRIND A COFFEE BEAN TO FINISH!

1977
01:14:59,294 --> 01:15:00,929
-Eight. Seven.
-Eight. Seven.

1978
01:15:00,996 --> 01:15:03,499
-That bread is still hot.
-Six. Five.

1979
01:15:03,565 --> 01:15:05,901
-Four. Three.
-Four. Three.

1980
01:15:05,968 --> 01:15:07,769
-Two. One.
-Two. One.

1981
01:15:07,836 --> 01:15:08,971
-Time's up.
-It's over.

1982
01:15:09,037 --> 01:15:11,173
He finished with <i>cervelas en </i>brioche.

1983
01:15:11,240 --> 01:15:13,141
-That was art.
-That was crazy.

1984
01:15:13,208 --> 01:15:14,776
That's crazy. They're so different.

1985
01:15:14,843 --> 01:15:18,380
FOOD ART, COMING RIGHT IN

1986
01:15:19,448 --> 01:15:20,449
Wow.

1987
01:15:20,516 --> 01:15:21,750
-You're so lucky.
-What?

1988
01:15:21,817 --> 01:15:24,386
-What do you mean?
-"Lucky."

1989
01:15:24,453 --> 01:15:28,290
Everything has been prepared
for CODE KUNST.

1990
01:15:28,357 --> 01:15:30,325
-They both look pretty.
-Right?

1991
01:15:30,392 --> 01:15:33,829
-Yes.
-Chef Ho-young finished cooking first.

1992
01:15:33,896 --> 01:15:35,497
<i>Such Warm Fruit, Warm Fruit</i>

1993
01:15:35,564 --> 01:15:37,399
<i>Such Warm Fruit, Warm Fruit</i>

1994
01:15:37,799 --> 01:15:40,302
WARM PEACH MEETS SWEET BANANA CUSTARD

1995
01:15:40,736 --> 01:15:42,404
BANANA CUSTARD

1996
01:15:42,471 --> 01:15:43,705
BANANA BRÛLÉE

1997
01:15:43,772 --> 01:15:46,341
FRIED PINEAPPLES

1998
01:15:47,042 --> 01:15:50,212
CHEF JUNG HO-YOUNG'S
SUCH WARM FRUIT, WARM FRUIT

1999
01:15:50,812 --> 01:15:52,114
Please try his dish.

2000
01:15:52,180 --> 01:15:53,348
STANDING AWKWARDLY

2001
01:15:53,415 --> 01:15:54,850
You can stay in your seat.

2002
01:15:56,084 --> 01:15:57,819
-I wanted to smell it.
-To smell it?

2003
01:15:57,886 --> 01:15:58,787
SNIFF

2004
01:15:59,288 --> 01:16:00,789
He can't sit still.

2005
01:16:00,856 --> 01:16:03,258
Make sure you count
and see if anything's missing.

2006
01:16:03,325 --> 01:16:05,894
-Yes.
-Huh? One, two, three…

2007
01:16:05,961 --> 01:16:06,795
LOL

2008
01:16:06,862 --> 01:16:09,097
-You seem rather jealous.
-I really am.

2009
01:16:09,164 --> 01:16:10,299
How should he eat this?

2010
01:16:10,365 --> 01:16:12,701
Take a piece of grilled peach

2011
01:16:12,768 --> 01:16:17,372
and soak it with the sauce,
a mix of cream cheese and custard.

2012
01:16:17,439 --> 01:16:21,543
That horseshoe was covered up.

2013
01:16:21,610 --> 01:16:23,779
You covered it with cream.

2014
01:16:23,845 --> 01:16:25,480
-I'll try it now.
-Sure.

2015
01:16:25,948 --> 01:16:27,950
IN ONE BITE

2016
01:16:28,650 --> 01:16:30,218
Did you make that custard with bananas?

2017
01:16:30,285 --> 01:16:32,120
AUTOMATIC SMILE

2018
01:16:32,588 --> 01:16:35,524
HAPPINESS SYNCING

2019
01:16:35,591 --> 01:16:36,858
He's looking happy again.

2020
01:16:36,925 --> 01:16:38,627
This sauce is really good.

2021
01:16:38,694 --> 01:16:40,128
PLEASED

2022
01:16:40,195 --> 01:16:41,863
JUST THE CUSTARD SAUCE

2023
01:16:41,930 --> 01:16:46,735
<i>I made it by mixing</i>
<i>banana custard and cream cheese.</i>

2024
01:16:46,802 --> 01:16:49,171
How's the peach?
Have you ever had it grilled?

2025
01:16:49,738 --> 01:16:51,540
It's my first time.

2026
01:16:53,075 --> 01:16:54,109
It's a little bitter.

2027
01:16:54,176 --> 01:16:55,410
-It is?
-It's bitter.

2028
01:16:55,477 --> 01:16:56,979
The horseshoe!

2029
01:16:57,279 --> 01:16:58,280
HORSESHOE EMERGENCY

2030
01:16:58,347 --> 01:16:59,481
We told you it was burnt.

2031
01:16:59,548 --> 01:17:02,117
-But the sauce is sweet, so it's fine.
-It's fine?

2032
01:17:02,184 --> 01:17:04,987
I always eat
something sweet with something bitter.

2033
01:17:05,053 --> 01:17:06,755
-Coffee.
-He drinks coffee too.

2034
01:17:06,822 --> 01:17:09,124
-Yes, he loves coffee.
-Like coffee.

2035
01:17:09,191 --> 01:17:12,027
Yes, during the process,

2036
01:17:12,094 --> 01:17:16,365
I wanted to think of a way
to express coffee through my dish.

2037
01:17:16,431 --> 01:17:18,400
-So I charred them on purpose.
-Charred.

2038
01:17:18,467 --> 01:17:19,935
-Intentionally.
-What's with our show?

2039
01:17:20,002 --> 01:17:22,704
-But it's good.
-It's… Is it?

2040
01:17:22,771 --> 01:17:25,273
-Charred.
-Yes, sweet custard

2041
01:17:25,340 --> 01:17:27,609
with slightly charred peach.

2042
01:17:27,676 --> 01:17:29,077
-That looks good.
-Next.

2043
01:17:29,144 --> 01:17:31,279
Something's down there.

2044
01:17:31,346 --> 01:17:32,881
-Grilled banana.
-I see.

2045
01:17:32,948 --> 01:17:33,782
The steaming sauce.

2046
01:17:33,849 --> 01:17:36,151
I think this is even better
because I'm eating it last.

2047
01:17:36,218 --> 01:17:38,820
-I got to eat my meals first.
-That's true.

2048
01:17:38,887 --> 01:17:41,023
-A nice ending.
-Good call.

2049
01:17:41,089 --> 01:17:42,224
After the crab dishes.

2050
01:17:42,290 --> 01:17:43,892
BANANA BRÛLÉE WITH BANANA CUSTARD

2051
01:17:43,959 --> 01:17:46,061
He said he wasn't a fan
of grilled bananas.

2052
01:17:46,128 --> 01:17:47,929
-He did.
-This one

2053
01:17:47,996 --> 01:17:50,165
is like a dessert you can get out there.

2054
01:17:51,533 --> 01:17:53,468
-I can taste caramel.
-It's sugar.

2055
01:17:53,535 --> 01:17:57,472
When you toast it and cool it,
it hardens like that.

2056
01:17:57,539 --> 01:17:58,807
<i>That looks really good.</i>

2057
01:17:59,841 --> 01:18:02,010
-Gosh.
-It's just grilled.

2058
01:18:02,077 --> 01:18:05,080
-You're going to love this.
-Really?

2059
01:18:06,248 --> 01:18:08,150
That's not very helpful.

2060
01:18:08,216 --> 01:18:10,185
-I'll try the fried pineapples.
-Yes, okay.

2061
01:18:10,252 --> 01:18:12,854
-Jung-hwan said it was hot. Be careful.
-So hot.

2062
01:18:14,156 --> 01:18:17,325
It was like drinking warm pineapple juice.

2063
01:18:18,460 --> 01:18:19,494
Huh?

2064
01:18:19,561 --> 01:18:21,596
It's just the flavor I love.

2065
01:18:21,663 --> 01:18:23,365
-Warm pineapple.
-The warm pineapple taste.

2066
01:18:23,432 --> 01:18:24,733
He said he liked that.

2067
01:18:24,800 --> 01:18:26,768
Ho-young keeps getting many points.

2068
01:18:26,835 --> 01:18:28,470
There's something else.

2069
01:18:28,537 --> 01:18:30,338
-Plum.
-Plum.

2070
01:18:30,405 --> 01:18:33,041
You don't have to eat that.

2071
01:18:33,108 --> 01:18:35,277
PUTTING IT BACK

2072
01:18:35,343 --> 01:18:36,178
It's just a decoration?

2073
01:18:36,244 --> 01:18:38,346
-Yes, it was to add color.
-Color.

2074
01:18:38,547 --> 01:18:40,148
SO MANY EXCUSES

2075
01:18:40,215 --> 01:18:42,517
Try the banana with the pineapple.

2076
01:18:42,584 --> 01:18:44,352
-Like this.
-It must be good. Sweet and sour.

2077
01:18:44,419 --> 01:18:45,353
It looks great.

2078
01:18:45,420 --> 01:18:48,623
-He's eating a lot today.
-That was a hearty bite.

2079
01:18:49,624 --> 01:18:51,393
He was a big eater.

2080
01:18:51,460 --> 01:18:52,728
Right?

2081
01:18:52,794 --> 01:18:54,396
It's even better like this.

2082
01:18:54,463 --> 01:18:58,366
-Mixed?
-Yes, because the banana is a bit sticky,

2083
01:18:58,433 --> 01:19:01,336
but the pineapple gives out juice,

2084
01:19:01,403 --> 01:19:02,370
so it makes it better.

2085
01:19:02,437 --> 01:19:03,772
It must be refreshing.

2086
01:19:04,773 --> 01:19:07,075
All right.
Please toss it to the other side.

2087
01:19:07,142 --> 01:19:10,112
Moving on to Chef Jong-won's dish.

2088
01:19:10,178 --> 01:19:11,646
PLUM COMPOTE

2089
01:19:11,713 --> 01:19:13,381
CERVELAS EN BRIOCHE

2090
01:19:13,448 --> 01:19:17,452
DICED PLUM

2091
01:19:17,519 --> 01:19:18,820
PEACH SALAD

2092
01:19:19,654 --> 01:19:22,724
COFFEE COCKTAIL WITH SWEET COFFEE AROMA

2093
01:19:23,358 --> 01:19:26,828
CHEF SON JONG-WON'S CAN YOU STOP MY PLUMS?

2094
01:19:27,763 --> 01:19:29,431
-What are you pulling out?
-I have sauce.

2095
01:19:29,498 --> 01:19:30,832
-Sauce.
-You have sauce?

2096
01:19:30,899 --> 01:19:32,968
-It's warm.
-He kept it warm.

2097
01:19:33,034 --> 01:19:34,536
So he had something more.

2098
01:19:34,603 --> 01:19:38,006
When you grill fruits,
it enhances the flavor and the aroma.

2099
01:19:38,073 --> 01:19:40,542
-So I wanted you to enjoy that.
-When it's grilled?

2100
01:19:40,609 --> 01:19:41,943
-So…
-What's that sauce?

2101
01:19:42,010 --> 01:19:43,979
<i>-I grilled and juiced the plums.</i>
<i>-The one from earlier.</i>

2102
01:19:44,045 --> 01:19:46,014
<i>I grilled them.</i>

2103
01:19:46,081 --> 01:19:48,650
He brings the fruit sauce

2104
01:19:48,717 --> 01:19:51,052
and pours it on top
of the <i>cervelas en </i>brioche.

2105
01:19:51,119 --> 01:19:52,120
How should he eat it?

2106
01:19:52,187 --> 01:19:55,524
First, go for the top left,
the sauce Ho-jin gave you.

2107
01:19:55,590 --> 01:19:57,392
-Put that on the bread.
-The bread.

2108
01:19:57,626 --> 01:19:59,694
STARING

2109
01:20:00,595 --> 01:20:01,696
Excuse me.

2110
01:20:01,763 --> 01:20:03,265
Your neck looks stiff.

2111
01:20:03,331 --> 01:20:04,366
STIFF

2112
01:20:04,432 --> 01:20:07,435
-It's a historic moment.
-A historic moment.

2113
01:20:07,936 --> 01:20:09,404
CUTS IN HALF

2114
01:20:09,471 --> 01:20:11,439
This one, Ho-jin sauce.

2115
01:20:11,506 --> 01:20:14,142
Warm fruit with <i>cervelas en </i>brioche.

2116
01:20:14,643 --> 01:20:16,978
A BIG BITE

2117
01:20:17,312 --> 01:20:18,547
CRUNCH

2118
01:20:18,613 --> 01:20:20,348
It sounds crispy.

2119
01:20:20,415 --> 01:20:23,118
-It must smell great.
-Yes. Is it hot?

2120
01:20:23,185 --> 01:20:24,820
-No, it's so good.
-It looks good.

2121
01:20:24,886 --> 01:20:26,054
It's so good?

2122
01:20:26,121 --> 01:20:28,423
The Ho-jin sauce is really good.

2123
01:20:28,490 --> 01:20:30,325
Ho-jin, this is good, thanks to you!

2124
01:20:30,392 --> 01:20:33,228
-Ho-jin!
-Thanks, Ho-jin.

2125
01:20:33,295 --> 01:20:36,398
-He's in middle school now.
-What's this? Plum?

2126
01:20:36,464 --> 01:20:39,534
Plum and apricot jam,
because sour fruits go well together.

2127
01:20:39,601 --> 01:20:41,203
<i>Yes, when I ordered bread,</i>

2128
01:20:41,269 --> 01:20:45,373
I had to add a tiny apricot jam
to reach the minimum amount.

2129
01:20:45,440 --> 01:20:48,210
You're finally trying
Ho-jin's quince <i>cheong,</i> right?

2130
01:20:48,276 --> 01:20:51,146
-It was quince <i>cheong?</i>
-It was.

2131
01:20:51,213 --> 01:20:52,948
He didn't even know that.

2132
01:20:53,014 --> 01:20:55,050
-I thought it was lemon.
-I see.

2133
01:20:55,116 --> 01:20:58,320
Next, cut the grilled peach
and salad in half.

2134
01:20:58,386 --> 01:20:59,788
I think I can have it in a bite.

2135
01:20:59,855 --> 01:21:01,590
FULL OF CONFIDENCE

2136
01:21:01,656 --> 01:21:03,792
At once? He's more confident now.

2137
01:21:03,859 --> 01:21:05,460
He won't even cut it.

2138
01:21:07,829 --> 01:21:08,930
He is.

2139
01:21:08,997 --> 01:21:10,732
This place is great.

2140
01:21:10,799 --> 01:21:12,300
I get compliments for eating.

2141
01:21:14,102 --> 01:21:16,071
We compliment you if you eat well.

2142
01:21:16,137 --> 01:21:17,739
It's so easy to get compliments.

2143
01:21:18,306 --> 01:21:20,675
-We love people who eat well.
-Gosh.

2144
01:21:20,742 --> 01:21:22,244
That looks good.

2145
01:21:22,310 --> 01:21:23,578
He eats well.

2146
01:21:23,645 --> 01:21:26,748
It must feel very refreshing.

2147
01:21:27,482 --> 01:21:28,750
TOUGH TO WATCH…

2148
01:21:28,817 --> 01:21:31,019
What's this? Apples?

2149
01:21:31,086 --> 01:21:32,220
-Plums.
-Plums.

2150
01:21:32,420 --> 01:21:33,622
YUM

2151
01:21:34,256 --> 01:21:36,224
THE FROWN OF TRUTH

2152
01:21:36,291 --> 01:21:38,827
Why does it taste like apricot?

2153
01:21:38,894 --> 01:21:40,095
I added a bit of apricot jam.

2154
01:21:41,096 --> 01:21:42,998
-His sense of taste is right on.
-It is.

2155
01:21:43,064 --> 01:21:44,432
I have good taste.

2156
01:21:44,499 --> 01:21:46,234
-You do.
-You're a gourmet.

2157
01:21:46,468 --> 01:21:48,436
HAPPY

2158
01:21:49,137 --> 01:21:51,139
I'll be too excited to sleep tonight.

2159
01:21:52,173 --> 01:21:54,009
He wasn't just good with coffee.

2160
01:21:54,075 --> 01:21:55,377
That's why he's a certified taster.

2161
01:21:55,443 --> 01:21:58,480
Most flavors
for the certification are fruity.

2162
01:22:00,682 --> 01:22:02,684
-The certification gets brought up again.
-He has a good sense.

2163
01:22:02,751 --> 01:22:05,453
-Next, try the cocktail.
-The cocktail.

2164
01:22:05,520 --> 01:22:07,289
-Coffee.
-The coffee martini.

2165
01:22:07,355 --> 01:22:09,257
-He'll be very objective.
-The martini.

2166
01:22:09,324 --> 01:22:12,260
He's a certified taster.
Let's hear from him.

2167
01:22:13,295 --> 01:22:14,562
ONE MORE SIP

2168
01:22:15,730 --> 01:22:16,898
It's really good.

2169
01:22:17,532 --> 01:22:20,201
I can't hold my alcohol,

2170
01:22:20,268 --> 01:22:22,170
so I like how it's not too strong.

2171
01:22:22,237 --> 01:22:24,005
-So it's not too strong.
-I like that about it.

2172
01:22:24,072 --> 01:22:27,776
And I can only smell the coffee.
It doesn't have any of the bitterness.

2173
01:22:27,842 --> 01:22:29,911
What was that beverage you used?

2174
01:22:29,978 --> 01:22:32,781
I sprinkled a dash
of chocolate protein powder.

2175
01:22:32,847 --> 01:22:34,716
-That too?
-That makes this sweet.

2176
01:22:34,783 --> 01:22:36,484
That's why it's sweet.

2177
01:22:36,551 --> 01:22:38,520
When I grab a drink,

2178
01:22:39,087 --> 01:22:41,189
I always have
an iced Americano on the side.

2179
01:22:41,256 --> 01:22:43,491
-Do you go back and forth?
-I like to do that.

2180
01:22:43,558 --> 01:22:45,994
-It tastes just like that.
-How did he know?

2181
01:22:46,061 --> 01:22:47,929
-He has an accurate taste.
-A good sense.

2182
01:22:47,996 --> 01:22:50,332
-He's sensitive to taste.
-His sense of taste.

2183
01:22:50,398 --> 01:22:51,800
You're right on.

2184
01:22:52,534 --> 01:22:54,002
Gosh.

2185
01:22:54,069 --> 01:22:56,137
It was even better because it was fruit.

2186
01:22:57,872 --> 01:22:59,874
-May I try some?
-Yes, it's your turn.

2187
01:22:59,941 --> 01:23:02,677
It's finally Seon-min's turn.

2188
01:23:02,744 --> 01:23:06,214
-That's Ho-young's dish.
-He'll have it in a bite.

2189
01:23:06,748 --> 01:23:08,016
It's that sauce.

2190
01:23:08,083 --> 01:23:09,718
YUM

2191
01:23:10,719 --> 01:23:13,021
PUSHING IT TO ONE SIDE

2192
01:23:13,321 --> 01:23:15,623
THE DISHWASHER POWERS ON

2193
01:23:15,857 --> 01:23:17,592
A SATISFACTORY NOD

2194
01:23:17,826 --> 01:23:19,294
GULP…

2195
01:23:19,527 --> 01:23:20,829
STARING

2196
01:23:20,895 --> 01:23:21,730
Gosh.

2197
01:23:21,796 --> 01:23:23,398
-Banana sauce.
-Isn't the sauce amazing?

2198
01:23:24,632 --> 01:23:26,901
FIDDLING WITH THE SPOON…

2199
01:23:26,968 --> 01:23:29,704
And that bitterness you talked about.

2200
01:23:29,771 --> 01:23:30,972
I don't taste it.

2201
01:23:31,039 --> 01:23:33,074
It's just a bit bittersweet.

2202
01:23:33,141 --> 01:23:35,977
It feels a little bit like
I'm chewing on bellflower root.

2203
01:23:36,211 --> 01:23:37,846
THE BIG EATER'S
EXCEPTIONAL SENSE OF TASTE

2204
01:23:37,912 --> 01:23:40,215
But it's really subtle.

2205
01:23:40,281 --> 01:23:42,751
-A tasty bitterness. It feels healthy.
-Healthy?

2206
01:23:42,817 --> 01:23:45,687
-It looks good.
-Fried pineapples.

2207
01:23:47,689 --> 01:23:48,923
MMM♥

2208
01:23:48,990 --> 01:23:49,824
Hmm?

2209
01:23:49,891 --> 01:23:51,126
What is it?

2210
01:23:51,192 --> 01:23:53,395
-Hmm?
-It must be so good.

2211
01:23:54,029 --> 01:23:55,430
Something suddenly hits me later.

2212
01:23:55,497 --> 01:23:57,198
Is that plum?

2213
01:23:57,265 --> 01:23:58,266
BRAIN LOADING

2214
01:23:58,333 --> 01:23:59,401
Pineapple.

2215
01:23:59,467 --> 01:24:01,669
-Yes, pineapple. It's pineapple.
-Yes.

2216
01:24:01,736 --> 01:24:03,471
At first, it's sweet.

2217
01:24:03,538 --> 01:24:06,674
<i>But at the end,</i>
<i>pineapple bursts out through the dredge.</i>

2218
01:24:06,741 --> 01:24:09,377
That bite had a perfect story.

2219
01:24:09,444 --> 01:24:10,645
-Because of the juice.
-Yes.

2220
01:24:10,712 --> 01:24:12,947
Next, Chef Jong-won's dish.

2221
01:24:13,014 --> 01:24:15,150
Chef Kyun has been holding his spoon.

2222
01:24:15,417 --> 01:24:16,885
WAITING QUIETLY

2223
01:24:16,951 --> 01:24:18,953
-Can you share a bite?
-I can usually…

2224
01:24:19,020 --> 01:24:21,589
I can usually get a bite

2225
01:24:21,656 --> 01:24:23,725
when I sit next to a guest.

2226
01:24:23,792 --> 01:24:26,061
-But this guy eats everything.
-And leaves nothing.

2227
01:24:26,561 --> 01:24:29,497
-I never got to use this spoon.
-You can use it now.

2228
01:24:29,764 --> 01:24:31,332
DISAPPOINTED(?)

2229
01:24:31,399 --> 01:24:33,168
He was holding it.
He could've shared some.

2230
01:24:33,234 --> 01:24:36,237
He usually calls the guests politely,

2231
01:24:36,304 --> 01:24:38,106
-but he called you "this guy."
-"This guy."

2232
01:24:38,173 --> 01:24:40,542
-I'm sorry.
-Share some.

2233
01:24:40,608 --> 01:24:41,910
Would you like one now?

2234
01:24:41,976 --> 01:24:43,278
It's all right.

2235
01:24:43,845 --> 01:24:46,548
Please try that one, Chef Eddie.

2236
01:24:46,614 --> 01:24:48,583
That one is Chef Jong-won's dish.

2237
01:24:50,018 --> 01:24:52,287
He made fruit sauce.

2238
01:24:52,554 --> 01:24:53,455
AMAZED EVERY TIME

2239
01:24:53,521 --> 01:24:54,856
He chews so quickly.

2240
01:24:54,923 --> 01:24:56,524
-He's like a grinder.
-So fast.

2241
01:24:57,492 --> 01:24:59,494
-Gosh.
-What's it like?

2242
01:24:59,561 --> 01:25:01,062
Me with my limited knowledge thought

2243
01:25:01,629 --> 01:25:04,332
bread and processed meat

2244
01:25:04,399 --> 01:25:06,167
wouldn't mix well together.

2245
01:25:07,402 --> 01:25:10,105
But it completely destroyed that thought.

2246
01:25:10,171 --> 01:25:12,040
-It's the sauce.
-Yes.

2247
01:25:12,107 --> 01:25:14,476
Who would've thought
it could be used for this?

2248
01:25:14,542 --> 01:25:15,944
Mmm.

2249
01:25:17,145 --> 01:25:18,813
-Do you want some?
-I'm fine.

2250
01:25:20,115 --> 01:25:21,816
PEACH SALAD

2251
01:25:22,250 --> 01:25:23,685
STARING

2252
01:25:23,751 --> 01:25:25,954
I think every chef in this studio

2253
01:25:26,020 --> 01:25:28,656
can create new kinds of flavors.

2254
01:25:28,723 --> 01:25:31,526
You have the ability
to create such a balanced taste.

2255
01:25:31,593 --> 01:25:33,828
-I was surprised by that too.
-Yes.

2256
01:25:33,895 --> 01:25:37,699
I never imagined you could add
fruit sauce to another fruit.

2257
01:25:37,765 --> 01:25:38,967
Yes, that.

2258
01:25:39,033 --> 01:25:40,401
That was such a wonder.

2259
01:25:40,969 --> 01:25:42,270
-This is great.
-Next.

2260
01:25:42,504 --> 01:25:44,906
FINISHING WITH THE COFFEE COCKTAIL

2261
01:25:48,343 --> 01:25:49,244
CHUCKLING

2262
01:25:49,310 --> 01:25:50,645
He likes it.

2263
01:25:51,212 --> 01:25:53,314
You had a drink,
so you should finish with this.

2264
01:25:53,381 --> 01:25:54,983
GLADLY

2265
01:25:56,251 --> 01:25:57,685
-This is good.
-That was good?

2266
01:25:57,752 --> 01:25:59,154
A SIP FIRST

2267
01:26:00,788 --> 01:26:03,024
-It has a nice texture.
-Nice texture.

2268
01:26:03,091 --> 01:26:05,793
-Ho-young's dish has a nice texture.
-Pineapple pieces.

2269
01:26:05,860 --> 01:26:07,262
They grilled peaches.

2270
01:26:07,328 --> 01:26:08,229
SHARING ONE BITE EACH

2271
01:26:09,130 --> 01:26:10,064
It's a close battle.

2272
01:26:10,131 --> 01:26:12,200
Yes, it's hard.

2273
01:26:12,267 --> 01:26:13,301
Gosh.

2274
01:26:13,368 --> 01:26:14,502
That's Jong-won's dish.

2275
01:26:15,603 --> 01:26:16,905
SLIDE

2276
01:26:16,971 --> 01:26:17,839
LAUGHING

2277
01:26:17,906 --> 01:26:19,707
It's like you're giving me prison grub!

2278
01:26:19,774 --> 01:26:20,608
Or dog food.

2279
01:26:22,043 --> 01:26:23,244
HERE, TAKE IT

2280
01:26:23,311 --> 01:26:26,080
-I wanted to push it to you.
-Giving it to him like that…

2281
01:26:26,147 --> 01:26:27,882
-Woof!
-Excuse me.

2282
01:26:27,949 --> 01:26:30,285
-Don't do that like he's an animal.
-I didn't.

2283
01:26:30,351 --> 01:26:32,453
He didn't want to get bitten.

2284
01:26:34,022 --> 01:26:36,958
DINING PEACEFULLY AMIDST THE RUCKUS

2285
01:26:37,025 --> 01:26:39,027
Chef Eddie, how do they compare?

2286
01:26:39,093 --> 01:26:39,994
They're great.

2287
01:26:40,061 --> 01:26:41,095
ACHOO!

2288
01:26:41,663 --> 01:26:42,964
That fruit…

2289
01:26:44,032 --> 01:26:47,468
He's trying to review the food.
Can you help us out?

2290
01:26:47,535 --> 01:26:49,370
SORRY AND SORRY

2291
01:26:49,437 --> 01:26:51,973
-How does he win stars?
-We're a classy talk show.

2292
01:26:52,040 --> 01:26:53,374
-Gosh.
-Good grief.

2293
01:26:53,441 --> 01:26:55,210
He was just talking.

2294
01:26:55,777 --> 01:26:56,978
-This is good.
-I'm sorry.

2295
01:26:57,045 --> 01:26:59,314
So pineapples go well with bananas.

2296
01:27:00,014 --> 01:27:01,082
Mmm.

2297
01:27:01,149 --> 01:27:03,284
Jong-won

2298
01:27:03,351 --> 01:27:06,988
used the fruits adequately
and created classy flavors.

2299
01:27:07,055 --> 01:27:08,289
It was fabulous.

2300
01:27:08,356 --> 01:27:09,891
-Wow.
-It tastes classy.

2301
01:27:09,958 --> 01:27:10,892
And Ho-young?

2302
01:27:10,959 --> 01:27:13,127
The texture and the sweetness

2303
01:27:13,194 --> 01:27:15,230
were all mixed together, and I loved it.

2304
01:27:15,296 --> 01:27:17,198
-I see.
-So it had a good balance?

2305
01:27:17,265 --> 01:27:19,934
It's time to announce the result.

2306
01:27:20,668 --> 01:27:23,204
<i>Press one for Jong-won</i>

2307
01:27:23,271 --> 01:27:24,772
<i>and two for Ho-young.</i>

2308
01:27:26,441 --> 01:27:29,210
Make your decision, and press the button.

2309
01:27:29,510 --> 01:27:32,380
THINKING AND THINKING…

2310
01:27:33,248 --> 01:27:34,349
-I did.
-He did.

2311
01:27:34,415 --> 01:27:36,217
Who was chosen

2312
01:27:36,284 --> 01:27:38,286
for his warm fruit dish?

2313
01:27:38,353 --> 01:27:40,855
Let's find out the result. Show us!

2314
01:27:41,389 --> 01:27:44,025
Which warm fruit dish
did CODE KUNST like better?

2315
01:27:44,092 --> 01:27:45,793
Jong-won, the sole first place.

2316
01:27:45,860 --> 01:27:48,196
Will he seal his first place?

2317
01:27:48,263 --> 01:27:50,398
Or will it be Ho-young,

2318
01:27:50,465 --> 01:27:53,301
who'll tie for second place if he wins?

2319
01:27:53,368 --> 01:27:55,003
Who did CODE KUNST choose?

2320
01:27:55,069 --> 01:27:56,137
Which one is it?

2321
01:27:57,138 --> 01:27:58,072
Who's the winner?

2322
01:27:58,873 --> 01:27:59,774
Who is it?

2323
01:28:05,747 --> 01:28:07,482
SON JONG-WON WINS

2324
01:28:07,548 --> 01:28:08,549
Jong-won wins.

2325
01:28:08,783 --> 01:28:11,252
CHEF SON JONG-WON'S
CAN YOU STOP MY PLUMS? WINS

2326
01:28:11,319 --> 01:28:13,921
-Jong-won wins.
-Gosh. It was close.

2327
01:28:14,422 --> 01:28:16,457
It seemed very close.

2328
01:28:16,524 --> 01:28:17,925
It's a matter of his preference.

2329
01:28:17,992 --> 01:28:20,795
They were both good,
so I had to think a lot.

2330
01:28:20,862 --> 01:28:24,399
<i>But the mix of Ho-jin's sauce and plums</i>

2331
01:28:24,465 --> 01:28:26,934
created a nice flavor
when they came together.

2332
01:28:28,703 --> 01:28:30,271
Did the martini play a part?

2333
01:28:30,338 --> 01:28:31,506
A big part.

2334
01:28:31,572 --> 01:28:34,208
-It played a big part.
-He loves coffee.

2335
01:28:34,275 --> 01:28:37,412
Ho-young, you failed to record
five consecutive wins.

2336
01:28:37,478 --> 01:28:40,715
My wife and I'll get back
into a bad relationship, then.

2337
01:28:42,317 --> 01:28:45,253
I'll be fine
if I stay away from fruits for a while.

2338
01:28:46,554 --> 01:28:49,457
Please put a star on Chef Jong-won!

2339
01:28:50,291 --> 01:28:53,127
He continues to keep his first place!

2340
01:28:53,194 --> 01:28:56,164
-He now has 14 stars.
-You have so many stars.

2341
01:28:56,230 --> 01:28:58,132
-The sole first place.
-That's a lot.

2342
01:28:58,199 --> 01:29:00,401
-Congratulations!
-Congratulations!

2343
01:29:01,402 --> 01:29:03,738
The stars
of the Aichi-Nagoya 2026 Asian Games,

2344
01:29:03,805 --> 01:29:06,507
Oh Sang-uk, Huh Mi-mi,
and Biwesa Daniel Kashama!

2345
01:29:06,574 --> 01:29:09,043
You were the MVP of the Asian Games.

2346
01:29:09,110 --> 01:29:10,378
THE KOREAN MVP, OH SANG-UK

2347
01:29:10,445 --> 01:29:11,746
He's like a celebrity.

2348
01:29:11,813 --> 01:29:12,880
He's way too cool.

2349
01:29:12,947 --> 01:29:14,782
THE CELEBRITY FOR NATIONAL ATHLETES

2350
01:29:15,016 --> 01:29:16,584
OH SANG-UK'S FENCER HABIT?

2351
01:29:16,651 --> 01:29:18,453
I always take steps to catch the elevator.

2352
01:29:18,686 --> 01:29:20,121
LIKE THIS?

2353
01:29:20,321 --> 01:29:21,489
CAN I CATCH THE ELEVATOR?

2354
01:29:21,556 --> 01:29:22,924
-Did I miss it?
-You were too late.

2355
01:29:23,124 --> 01:29:24,459
HE'S A WORLD-CLASS FUNNY GUY TOO

2356
01:29:24,525 --> 01:29:26,828
Someone here is Sang-uk's family.

2357
01:29:27,061 --> 01:29:28,162
WHO COULD BE HIS FAMILY?

2358
01:29:28,229 --> 01:29:29,597
-Chef Sung-jun.
-What?

2359
01:29:29,664 --> 01:29:34,535
He's my grandfather's younger brother's
wife's older sister's son.

2360
01:29:34,602 --> 01:29:35,636
They're strangers.

2361
01:29:35,703 --> 01:29:36,571
-They are.
-That's…

2362
01:29:36,738 --> 01:29:38,573
FOR THE ATHLETES WHO RETURNED IN GLORY,

2363
01:29:38,840 --> 01:29:42,343
THE SHOW HOLDS ITS FIRST ITEM ROUND

2364
01:29:42,610 --> 01:29:44,579
BRINGING IN A SECRET WEAPON

2365
01:29:44,645 --> 01:29:45,813
FILLED WITH CURIOSITY

2366
01:29:46,013 --> 01:29:47,849
SON JONG-WON BROUGHT SOMETHING BIG!

2367
01:29:48,116 --> 01:29:49,283
WHAT'S THAT?

2368
01:29:49,484 --> 01:29:50,718
WHICH ITEM SHOCKED EVERYONE?

2369
01:29:50,885 --> 01:29:51,853
THE BEST CHEFS START COOKING

2370
01:29:52,053 --> 01:29:52,920
FOR THE NATIONAL ATHLETES

2371
01:29:52,987 --> 01:29:54,055
ALLEZ!

2372
01:29:54,389 --> 01:29:57,024
ALL KINDS OF ADVANCED TECHNIQUES

2373
01:29:57,191 --> 01:29:58,259
FENTE!

2374
01:29:58,426 --> 01:30:00,328
A SHARP STAB

2375
01:30:00,628 --> 01:30:02,263
LOVES IT

2376
01:30:02,530 --> 01:30:03,998
THE NATIONAL ATHLETE'S MUKBANG BEGINS

2377
01:30:04,065 --> 01:30:04,899
He's a big eater.

2378
01:30:04,966 --> 01:30:06,167
-Wow.
-He eats well.

2379
01:30:06,234 --> 01:30:07,835
-He does.
-Sang-uk the big eater.

2380
01:30:08,035 --> 01:30:09,971
A GOLD MEDAL FOR THE WAY HE EATS

2381
01:30:10,405 --> 01:30:12,240
WHICH DISH MADE HIM SHOW A THUMBS-UP?

2382
01:30:12,306 --> 01:30:13,307
It was absolutely delicious.

2383
01:30:15,610 --> 01:30:18,146
Mirror, mirror, on the wall.
Who do you think will win today?

2384
01:30:19,046 --> 01:30:20,415
Of course, it's Chef Eun-yeong.

2385
01:30:21,182 --> 01:30:23,451
If there were a tournament for lunatics,

2386
01:30:23,518 --> 01:30:24,585
she'd win the grand slam.

2387
01:30:34,629 --> 01:30:39,400
Subtitle translation by: Eunsook Youn
